OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026", "title": "A negatively pinched Kähler threefold without bounded holomorphic coordinates", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "359", "family_title": "Negative Kähler curvature without bounded holomorphic coordinates", "abstract_html": "We construct a contractible domain in complex dimension three with a complete Kähler metric whose real sectional curvatures lie between two finite negative constants. It admits no bounded holomorphic map to ℂ<sup>3</sup> with nowhere-vanishing Jacobian and is therefore not biholomorphic to a bounded domain. This gives a negative answer to the negatively pinched Kähler uniformization question.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist a contractible domain \\(M\\subset\\mathbb C^3\\), a smooth complete Kähler metric \\(g\\) on \\(M\\), and finite constants \\(0<A\\le B\\) such that \\( -B\\le K_g(\\sigma)\\le-A<0 \\) for every real two-plane …", "statement": "<p>There exist a contractible domain \\(M\\subset\\mathbb C^3\\), a smooth complete Kähler metric \\(g\\) on \\(M\\), and finite constants \\(0&lt;A\\le B\\) such that\n\\[\n-B\\le K_g(\\sigma)\\le-A&lt;0\n\\]\nfor every real two-plane \\(\\sigma\\subset T_pM\\) and every \\(p\\in M\\).</p><p>No bounded holomorphic map \\(F:M\\to\\mathbb C^3\\) has a nowhere-vanishing Jacobian determinant. In particular, \\(M\\) is not biholomorphic to a bounded domain in \\(\\mathbb C^3\\).</p><p>Here sectional curvature is the sectional curvature of the underlying real Riemannian metric, completeness means geodesic completeness, and a bounded holomorphic map is a holomorphic map with bounded Euclidean image.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\phi\\) from Proposition 4 and then choose \\(\\lambda\\) from Proposition 11. The domain\n\\[\nM=M_\\phi\\subset\\mathbb C^3\n\\]\nis contractible and carries the asserted smooth complete Kähler metric with two uniform negative real-sectional bounds.</p><p>Proposition 5 excludes a bounded holomorphic map \\(M\\to\\mathbb C^3\\) with nowhere-vanishing Jacobian. A biholomorphism to a bounded domain would be such a map: the chain rule applied to its holomorphic inverse makes its Jacobian invertible everywhere.</p><p>The logarithm argument uses nonvanishing along the entire zero section. It gives no assertion that a bounded map’s determinant vanishes identically. Nor are all bounded holomorphic functions constant: the base coordinates \\(z_1,z_2\\) are nonconstant bounded holomorphic functions on \\(M_\\phi\\).</p><p>Proposition 6 also supplies a smooth strictly plurisubharmonic exhaustion. As noted in the paper, Grauert’s solution of the Levi problem therefore makes this domain Stein; the obstruction is to bounded holomorphic coordinates, not to holomorphic convexity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For any fixed \\(1<a<2\\), with \\(\\alpha=\\log a\\), there exist \\(0<s_1<1\\), smooth real functions \\(f,b\\) on \\([0,\\infty)\\), and \\(R_0>0\\) such that \\( \\alpha^2-2s_1(\\cosh\\alpha-1)<0, \\) …", "statement": "<p>Separated homogeneous peaks and radial profiles; assembling original Lemmas 3.2–3.4.</p><p>Definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb C^2:|\\xi|=1\\}\\), and let \\(\\sigma\\) be normalized surface measure on \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The Hermitian inner product is linear in its first argument.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(\\mathbb{CP}^1\\), define\n\\[\nd([\\xi],[p])=\\sqrt{1-|\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle|^2}\n\\]\nfor unit representatives.</p><p class=\"li\">Given \\(D_0\\ge1\\) and an integer \\(k\\ge D_0^2\\), choose a maximal \\(D_0/\\sqrt{k}\\)-separated set of lines and one unit representative of each line. Denote the resulting set by \\(\\mathcal P_k\\), put \\(N_k=|\\mathcal P_k|\\), and define\n\\[\nP_k(z)=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P_k}\\langle z,p\\rangle^k.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For any fixed \\(1&lt;a&lt;2\\), with \\(\\alpha=\\log a\\), there exist \\(0&lt;s_1&lt;1\\), smooth real functions \\(f,b\\) on \\([0,\\infty)\\), and \\(R_0&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\alpha^2-2s_1(\\cosh\\alpha-1)&lt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_\\infty&lt;1,\\qquad\nf=-s_1\\ \\text{on }[0,\\alpha],\\qquad\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-y}f(y)\\,dy=0,\n\\]\n\\[\nb'-b=f,\\qquad b(0)=0,\n\\]\nand both functions vanish on a neighborhood of \\([R_0,\\infty)\\).</p><p>For these profiles, every\n\\[\nD_0&gt;\\max\\{1,2\\sqrt{2R_0}\\},\\qquad\nk\\ge\\max\\{D_0^2,2R_0\\},\n\\]\nand every choice of the separated representatives above have the following properties.</p><p>In unitary coordinates adapted to \\(p\\in\\mathcal P_k\\), write\n\\[\n\\xi=(e^{i\\theta}\\cos v,e^{i\\gamma}\\sin v),\\qquad\ny=-k\\log|\\xi_1|,\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nU_{k,p}=\\{\\xi\\in S:0\\le y&lt;R_0\\}.\n\\]\nThese patches are pairwise disjoint and invariant under common phase rotation. There are absolute constants \\(C,c&gt;0\\), independent of \\(D_0,R_0,k\\), such that\n\\[\n\\frac{k}{D_0^2}\\le N_k\\le\\frac{4k}{D_0^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{p\\in\\mathcal P_k\\\\ \\sqrt{k}\\,d(\\xi,p)\\ge u}}\n|\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle|^k\\le Ce^{-cu^2}\n\\qquad(\\xi\\in S,\\ u\\ge0),\n\\]\n\\[\n|P_k(z)|\\le C|z|^k\\quad(z\\in\\mathbb C^2),\\qquad\n\\int_S|P_k|\\,d\\sigma\\le\\frac{2N_k}{k+2}.\n\\]\nOn each patch,\n\\[\nd\\sigma=\\frac2k e^{-2y/k}\\,dy\\,\\frac{d\\theta}{2\\pi}\\frac{d\\gamma}{2\\pi},\n\\]\n\\[\n|P_k(\\xi)-e^{-y}e^{ik\\theta}|\n\\le Ce^{-c(D_0-\\sqrt{2R_0})^2}.\n\\]\nOutside the patches,\n\\[\n|P_k(\\xi)|\\le Ce^{-cR_0},\\qquad |aP_k(\\xi)|\\le\\frac12.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(s_1,f,b,R_0\\) by Lemma 1.3. For any admissible spacing \\(D_0\\), degree \\(k\\), and representatives, Lemma 1.1 gives the packing, Gaussian tail, and polynomial bounds; Lemma 1.2 gives the disjoint patches, measure formula, and single-peak approximation. The final choice of \\(R_0\\) in Lemma 1.3 supplies \\(|aP_k|\\le1/2\\) off the patches.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, together with the projective metric and measure calculation preceding it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the definitions of \\(S,\\sigma,d,\\mathcal P_k,N_k,P_k\\) in Proposition 1, but assume only \\(D_0\\ge1\\) and the integer condition \\(k\\ge D_0^2\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the definitions of \\(S,\\sigma,d,\\mathcal P_k,N_k,P_k\\) in Proposition 1, but assume only \\(D_0\\ge1\\) and the integer condition \\(k\\ge D_0^2\\).</p><p>Then \\(d\\) is a metric on \\(\\mathbb{CP}^1\\), a projective cap of radius \\(s\\in[0,1]\\) has measure \\(s^2\\), and there are absolute constants \\(C,c&gt;0\\), independent of \\(D_0\\), for which\n\\[\n\\frac{k}{D_0^2}\\le N_k\\le\\frac{4k}{D_0^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{p\\in\\mathcal P_k\\\\ \\sqrt{k}\\,d(\\xi,p)\\ge u}}\n|\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle|^k\\le Ce^{-cu^2}\n\\qquad(\\xi\\in S,\\ u\\ge0).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n|P_k(z)|\\le C|z|^k\\quad(z\\in\\mathbb C^2),\\qquad\n\\int_S|P_k|\\,d\\sigma\\le\\frac{2N_k}{k+2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The distance \\(d\\) equals the operator norm distance between the rank-one orthogonal projections onto the two lines: their difference has trace zero and eigenvalues\n\\[\n\\pm\\sqrt{1-|\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle|^2}.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality for the operator norm therefore proves that \\(d\\) is a metric.</p><p>In unitary coordinates with \\(\\xi_1=\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle\\), write\n\\[\n\\xi=(e^{i\\theta}\\cos v,e^{i\\gamma}\\sin v),\n\\qquad 0\\le v\\le\\pi/2.\n\\]\nThe round volume element is \\(\\cos v\\sin v\\,dv\\,d\\theta\\,d\\gamma\\). After normalization and the change of variable \\(u=\\cos^2v\\),\n\\[\nd\\sigma=du\\,\\frac{d\\theta}{2\\pi}\\frac{d\\gamma}{2\\pi},\n\\qquad 0\\le u\\le1.\n\\]\nIn particular, a projective cap \\(d(\\xi,p)&lt;s\\) has measure \\(s^2\\).</p><p>Put \\(\\rho=D_0/\\sqrt{k}\\le1\\). Maximality gives a covering by caps of radius \\(\\rho\\), and separation gives disjoint interiors for caps of radius \\(\\rho/2\\). Their measures yield\n\\[\n1\\le N_k\\rho^2,\\qquad N_k\\rho^2/4\\le1,\n\\]\nproving the asserted bounds for \\(N_k\\).</p><p>For a local count independent of \\(D_0\\), place caps of radius \\(1/(3\\sqrt{k})\\) about the centers within distance \\(u/\\sqrt{k}\\) of a fixed line. Their interiors are disjoint, and they lie inside the cap of radius \\((u+1/3)/\\sqrt{k}\\) about that line. Comparing measures bounds their number by \\(9(u+1/3)^2\\) when the latter radius is at most one. Otherwise their number is at most\n\\[\n9k\\le9(u+1/3)^2.\n\\]\nThus the local count is at most \\(C(1+u)^2\\) in all cases.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n|\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle|^k\n=(1-d(\\xi,p)^2)^{k/2}\n\\le e^{-kd(\\xi,p)^2/2},\n\\]\nthe contribution from the annulus\n\\[\nn\\le\\sqrt{k}\\,d(\\xi,p)&lt;n+1\n\\]\nis at most \\(C(n+2)^2e^{-n^2/2}\\). Summing from \\(n=\\lfloor u\\rfloor\\) onward proves the Gaussian tail estimate: the polynomial factor is absorbed by a weaker Gaussian exponent, and \\(0\\le u\\le2\\) is absorbed by the constant.</p><p>Setting \\(u=0\\) and using homogeneity gives the pointwise polynomial bound, including outside the unit ball. Finally,\n\\[\n\\int_S|\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle|^k\\,d\\sigma\n=\\int_0^1u^{k/2}\\,du=\\frac2{k+2}.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality proves the integral bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(R_0>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(R_0&gt;0\\). Assume\n\\[\nD_0&gt;\\max\\{1,2\\sqrt{2R_0}\\},\\qquad\nk\\ge\\max\\{D_0^2,2R_0\\},\n\\]\nand use the separated sets and patches defined in Proposition 1.</p><p>Then the patches \\(U_{k,p}\\) are pairwise disjoint. On each patch,\n\\[\nd\\sigma=\\frac2k e^{-2y/k}\\,dy\\,\n\\frac{d\\theta}{2\\pi}\\frac{d\\gamma}{2\\pi},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|P_k(\\xi)-e^{-y}e^{ik\\theta}|\n\\le Ce^{-c(D_0-\\sqrt{2R_0})^2}.\n\\]\nOutside their union,\n\\[\n|P_k(\\xi)|\\le Ce^{-cR_0}.\n\\]\nThe constants \\(C,c\\) can be chosen independently of \\(R_0,D_0,k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The projective radius of a patch is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1-e^{-2R_0/k}}\\le\\sqrt{2R_0/k},\n\\]\nso the separation of the centers makes the patches disjoint. The measure formula follows from \\(|\\xi_1|^2=e^{-2y/k}\\) in the measure calculation of Lemma 1.1.</p><p>If \\(\\xi\\in U_{k,p}\\) and \\(p'\\ne p\\) is another center, the metric triangle inequality gives\n\\[\n\\sqrt{k}\\,d(\\xi,p')\\ge D_0-\\sqrt{2R_0}.\n\\]\nThe peak from \\(p\\) is exactly \\(e^{-y}e^{ik\\theta}\\). Summing all other peaks by Lemma 1.1 proves the single-peak approximation.</p><p>Outside every patch, each center satisfies\n\\[\nkd(\\xi,p)^2\n\\ge k(1-e^{-2R_0/k})\\ge R_0.\n\\]\nThe final inequality uses \\(k\\ge2R_0\\) and \\(1-e^{-v}\\ge v/2\\) for \\(0\\le v\\le1\\). Another application of Lemma 1.1 proves the off-patch bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(1<a<2\\) and put \\(\\alpha=\\log a\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(1&lt;a&lt;2\\) and put \\(\\alpha=\\log a\\). There exist \\(0&lt;s_1&lt;1\\), smooth real functions \\(f,b\\) on \\([0,\\infty)\\), and \\(R_0&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\alpha^2-2s_1(\\cosh\\alpha-1)&lt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_\\infty&lt;1,\\qquad\nf=-s_1\\ \\text{on }[0,\\alpha],\\qquad\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-y}f(y)\\,dy=0,\n\\]\n\\[\nb'-b=f,\\qquad b(0)=0.\n\\]\nBoth functions vanish on a neighborhood of \\([R_0,\\infty)\\). Moreover, \\(R_0\\) can be chosen so that \\(|aP_k|\\le1/2\\) off the patches whenever the hypotheses of Lemma 1.2 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>The strict inequality\n\\[\n2(\\cosh\\alpha-1)&gt;\\alpha^2\n\\]\nallows a choice\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha^2}{2(\\cosh\\alpha-1)}&lt;s_1&lt;1.\n\\]\nSince \\(a&lt;2\\), this choice also satisfies\n\\[\ns_1(1-e^{-\\alpha})&lt;e^{-\\alpha}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose a small \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) and a smooth cutoff \\(\\chi\\), equal to one on \\([0,\\alpha]\\), between zero and one, and zero for \\(y\\ge\\alpha+\\delta\\). For sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\),\n\\[\nN=s_1\\int_0^\\infty e^{-y}\\chi(y)\\,dy\n&lt;e^{-(\\alpha+2\\delta)}.\n\\]\nChoose a smooth compactly supported function \\(\\nu\\), supported strictly after \\(\\alpha+2\\delta\\), with \\(0\\le\\nu\\le1\\), and with\n\\[\nP=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-y}\\nu(y)\\,dy&gt;N.\n\\]\nSuch a function is obtained by approximating the indicator of a sufficiently long interval beginning just after \\(\\alpha+2\\delta\\).</p><p>Then\n\\[\nf=-s_1\\chi+(N/P)\\nu\n\\]\nhas the required plateau and weighted integral. The supports are disjoint, so\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_\\infty\\le\\max\\{s_1,N/P\\}&lt;1.\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nb(y)=e^y\\int_0^y e^{-v}f(v)\\,dv.\n\\]\nIt satisfies \\(b'-b=f\\) and \\(b(0)=0\\). Beyond the support of \\(f\\), the integral is exactly zero by the weighted cancellation, so \\(b\\) also has compact support. Near zero,\n\\[\nb(y)=-s_1(e^y-1),\n\\]\nwhich is smooth up to the boundary of the half-line. Smoothness here does not require extending \\(f\\) by zero to negative arguments.</p><p>Finally choose \\(R_0\\) larger than both supports, and enlarge it further until\n\\[\naCe^{-cR_0}\\le\\frac12\n\\]\nin Lemma 1.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "uniform representing densities", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a constant \\(K_*>0\\), depending only on \\(f,b,R_0\\), such that for every fixed \\(D_0\\) above there is an integer threshold \\(Q_0\\) with the following property.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the profiles \\(f,b,R_0\\) of Proposition 1 and put\n\\[\nc_0=\\frac{1-\\|f\\|_\\infty}{4}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n\\[\nD_0&gt;\\max\\{1,2\\sqrt{2R_0}\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(Q\\ge2\\), sufficiently large that the patch conditions hold at every degree below, define\n\\[\nk_j=Q^j,\\qquad \\ell_0=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_j=\\sum_{l=1}^j k_l.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\ell_{j-1}&lt;k_j\\) and \\(\\ell_j&lt;2k_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At every degree use any separated set and patches supplied by Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(W_0=1\\). At stage \\(j\\), write \\(k=k_j\\), and for \\(p\\in\\mathcal P_k\\) put\n\\[\nS_p(\\theta)=W_{j-1}(e^{i\\theta}p).\n\\]\nOn \\(U_{k,p}\\), define\n\\[\nA_{j,p}(\\xi)\n=e^{2y/k}\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\left[\ne^{ik\\theta}\n\\left(f(y)S_p(\\theta)-b(y)\\frac{S_p'(\\theta)}{ik}\\right)\n\\right],\n\\]\nextend this correction by zero off its patch, and put\n\\[\nW_j=W_{j-1}+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P_k}A_{j,p}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the phase derivative by\n\\[\nTW(\\xi)=\\left.\\frac{d}{d\\omega}W(e^{i\\omega}\\xi)\\right|_{\\omega=0}.\n\\]\nA horizontal vector \\(Z\\in T_\\xi S\\) means a Euclidean unit real tangent vector satisfying \\(\\langle Z,\\xi\\rangle=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A function has phase bandwidth at most \\(\\ell\\) if its restriction to every orbit \\(\\omega\\mapsto e^{i\\omega}\\xi\\) is a trigonometric polynomial with frequencies in \\([-\\ell,\\ell]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A probability measure \\(\\mu\\) on \\(S\\) represents holomorphic evaluation at zero if\n\\[\n\\int_Sp(\\xi)\\,d\\mu(\\xi)=p(0)\n\\]\nfor every holomorphic polynomial \\(p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(K_*&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(f,b,R_0\\), such that for every fixed \\(D_0\\) above there is an integer threshold \\(Q_0\\) with the following property. For every integer \\(Q\\ge Q_0\\), every choice of the separated sets, and every \\(j\\ge1\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(W_j\\) is smooth and strictly positive, and \\(W_j\\,d\\sigma\\) represents holomorphic evaluation at zero; in particular, it is a probability measure.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(W_j\\) has phase bandwidth at most \\(\\ell_j\\), and its mean on every phase orbit is exactly one.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. At every point,\n\\[\nc_0W_{j-1}\\le W_j\\le2W_{j-1},\n\\]\n\\[\n|ZW_j|\\le K_*\\sqrt{k_j}\\,W_j,\\qquad\n|TW_j|\\le K_*k_jW_j.\n\\]</p><p>The threshold \\(Q_0\\) may be enlarged to impose any fixed lower threshold on all degrees \\(k_j\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2 prove smoothness, exact moments, phase bandwidth, and orbitwise mean independently of positivity. It remains to establish positivity and the uniform derivative estimates with one choice of \\(Q\\).</p><p>Suppose the estimates hold through stage \\(j-1\\), and write \\(k=k_j\\). Combining Lemmas 2.3 and 2.4 with the bandwidth assertion gives\n\\[\n\\frac{|S_p'|}{kS_p}\\le\\frac{K_*}{Q},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{|S_p''|}{k^2S_p}\\le\\frac{C_1}{Q^2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_1\\) depends only on \\(K_*,c_0\\), and the fixed cutoff in Lemma 2.4. It is independent of \\(Q,j,p\\). For \\(j\\ge2\\), apply those lemmas with \\(q=k_{j-1}\\), noting \\(\\ell_{j-1}\\le2k_{j-1}\\). For \\(j=1\\), both derivatives vanish because \\(S_p=1\\).</p><p>Fix\n\\[\nK_*&gt;\\frac{2C_2}{c_0},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_2\\) is from Lemma 2.5. The constant \\(C_1\\) is now fixed.</p><p>The previous horizontal derivative bound, integrated along the radial arc in Lemma 2.5, implies\n\\[\ne^{-K_*D_R/\\sqrt Q}\n\\le\\frac{S_p(\\theta)}{W_{j-1}(\\xi)}\n\\le e^{K_*D_R/\\sqrt Q}.\n\\]\nIndeed, the logarithmic derivative along a unit horizontal arc is at most \\(K_*\\sqrt{k_{j-1}}\\), and the arc length is at most \\(D_R/\\sqrt{k_j}\\). At the initial stage the ratio is exactly one.</p><p>Since \\(k_j\\ge Q\\), put\n\\[\nH(Q)=\\exp\\!\\left(K_*D_R/\\sqrt Q+2R_0/Q\\right).\n\\]\nChoose \\(Q_0\\) so that every integer \\(Q\\ge Q_0\\) satisfies all degree and patch requirements and also\n\\[\nQ\\ge2,\\qquad H(Q)\\le2,\\qquad K_*/Q\\le1,\\qquad C_1/Q^2\\le1,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ^{-1/2}\\le c_0/2,\\qquad\nH(Q)(F+BK_*/Q)\\le1-2c_0.\n\\]\nThese requirements are compatible because \\(H(Q)\\to1\\) and\n\\[\nF=1-4c_0&lt;1-2c_0.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(Q\\ge Q_0\\) and induct on \\(j\\). The preceding estimates establish the normalized hypotheses of Lemma 2.5. Moreover,\n\\[\n|A_{j,p}|\n\\le e^{2y/k}S_p(F+Br_1)\n\\le H(Q)(F+BK_*/Q)W_{j-1}\n\\le(1-2c_0)W_{j-1}.\n\\]\nAt any point at most one patch correction is nonzero. Hence\n\\[\n2c_0W_{j-1}\\le W_j\\le(2-2c_0)W_{j-1},\n\\]\nwhich proves positivity and the required multiplicative comparison.</p><p>For \\(Y=Z/\\sqrt{k_j}\\), the preceding derivative bound gives\n\\[\n|YW_{j-1}|\\le(K_*/\\sqrt Q)W_{j-1}.\n\\]\nFor \\(Y=T/k_j\\), it gives the smaller coefficient \\(K_*/Q\\). Together with Lemma 2.5 and the choices above,\n\\[\n|YW_j|\n\\le(C_2+K_*/\\sqrt Q)W_{j-1}\n\\le K_*c_0W_{j-1}\\le K_*W_j.\n\\]\nOutside the patches the correction vanishes. Its smooth zero extension gives the same estimate on patch boundaries. Multiplication by \\(\\sqrt{k_j}\\) or \\(k_j\\) proves the derivative bounds and completes the induction.</p><p>By Lemma 2.2, \\(\\int_SW_j\\,d\\sigma=1\\); positivity therefore makes \\(W_j\\,d\\sigma\\) a representing probability.</p><p>The order of choices is \\(C_2\\), then \\(K_*\\), then \\(C_1\\), and finally \\(Q_0\\). No lower bound for \\(W_j\\) uniform in \\(j\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the recursion and patch hypotheses of Proposition 2, without assuming positivity of any density.", "statement": "<p>Use the recursion and patch hypotheses of Proposition 2, without assuming positivity of any density. Every \\(W_j\\) defined by that recursion is a smooth real function on \\(S\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proceed by induction, beginning with \\(W_0=1\\). The restriction \\(S_p\\) is a smooth periodic function.</p><p>To check the central circle of a patch, use the smooth coordinates\n\\[\n(\\theta,\\zeta)\\longmapsto\n(e^{i\\theta}\\sqrt{1-|\\zeta|^2},\\zeta),\n\\qquad\ny=-\\frac{k}{2}\\log(1-|\\zeta|^2).\n\\]\nAll terms of the correction are smooth in these coordinates, including at \\(\\zeta=0\\). They do not depend on the transverse polar angle \\(\\gamma\\), which would be singular there. Their dependence on \\(\\theta\\) is periodic, so it defines a smooth function along the full central circle.</p><p>At the outer boundary, \\(f\\) and \\(b\\) vanish in a collar of \\(R_0\\); hence the correction is identically zero in a collar of the patch boundary. Its extension by zero is therefore smooth. Summing the finitely many corrections proves the induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the recursion of Proposition 2, without assuming positivity.", "statement": "<p>Use the recursion of Proposition 2, without assuming positivity. For every stage \\(j\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname{bandwidth}(W_j)\\le\\ell_j,\n\\]\nthe mean of \\(W_j\\) on every phase orbit is one, and\n\\[\n\\int_Sp(\\xi)W_j(\\xi)\\,d\\sigma(\\xi)=p(0)\n\\]\nfor every holomorphic polynomial \\(p\\).</p><p>Each individual correction \\(A_{j,p}\\) has zero integral against every holomorphic polynomial, including the constant polynomial.</p>", "proof": "<p>At stage zero the phase assertions are immediate. The polynomial identity for \\(\\sigma\\) follows by global phase averaging: every nonconstant homogeneous holomorphic polynomial has nonzero positive phase frequency.</p><p>Assume the assertions through stage \\(j-1\\), and write \\(k=k_j\\). The central restriction has a finite expansion\n\\[\nS_p(\\theta)=\\sum_{|s|\\le\\ell_{j-1}}\\widehat S_p(s)e^{is\\theta},\n\\qquad \\widehat S_p(0)=1.\n\\]\nSince \\(b'-b=f\\), the bracket in the correction is\n\\[\n\\sum_s\\widehat S_p(s)\n\\left(b'-\\frac{k+s}{k}b\\right)e^{is\\theta}.\n\\]\nAll integers \\(m=k+s\\) are positive because \\(\\ell_{j-1}&lt;k\\). The patch and \\(y\\) are invariant under global phase rotation, whereas \\(\\theta\\) increases by the rotation angle. Hence the correction has frequencies in\n\\[\n[k-\\ell_{j-1},k+\\ell_{j-1}]\n\\cup[-k-\\ell_{j-1},-k+\\ell_{j-1}].\n\\]\nThey omit zero. Multiplication by a function of \\(y\\), and extension by zero across the invariant patch boundary, do not alter these frequencies. The bandwidth of \\(W_j\\) is therefore at most \\(k+\\ell_{j-1}=\\ell_j\\), and its orbitwise mean remains one.</p><p>For the full moment calculation, use an adapted monomial \\(\\xi_1^n\\xi_2^l\\), where \\(n,l\\) are nonnegative integers. If \\(l&gt;0\\), averaging in \\(\\gamma\\) gives zero. If \\(l=n=0\\), averaging in \\(\\theta\\) gives zero because all \\(m=k+s\\) are positive.</p><p>Suppose \\(l=0\\) and \\(n\\ge1\\). As \\(b\\) is real,\n\\[\nA_{j,p}\n=\\frac{e^{2y/k}}2\\sum_s\n\\left(\n\\widehat S_p(s)e^{i(k+s)\\theta}\n+\\overline{\\widehat S_p(s)}e^{-i(k+s)\\theta}\n\\right)\n\\left(b'-\\frac{k+s}{k}b\\right).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\xi_1^n=e^{-ny/k}e^{in\\theta}\\), only the conjugate frequency with \\(k+s=n\\) can survive the \\(\\theta\\)-integration. The patch measure gives exactly\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{U_{k,p}}\\xi_1^nA_{j,p}\\,d\\sigma\n&amp;=\\frac{\\overline{\\widehat S_p(n-k)}}k\n\\int_0^{R_0}e^{-ny/k}\\left(b'-\\frac nk b\\right)\\,dy\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{\\overline{\\widehat S_p(n-k)}}k\n\\left[e^{-ny/k}b(y)\\right]_0^{R_0}=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nA coefficient outside the previous bandwidth is zero. The factor \\(2/k\\) in surface measure combines with the \\(1/2\\) from the real part to leave \\(1/k\\). Both endpoint terms vanish because \\(b(0)=b(R_0)=0\\).</p><p>The argument applies to every degree \\(n\\), without a degree cutoff. Unitary changes of coordinates preserve holomorphic polynomials, so each correction annihilates every polynomial in the original coordinates. Adding the corrections proves the moment identity and completes the induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(Q\\ge2\\), \\(k_j=Q^j\\), and \\(n\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(Q\\ge2\\), \\(k_j=Q^j\\), and \\(n\\ge1\\). Suppose positive functions \\(W_0=1,W_1,\\ldots,W_n\\) satisfy\n\\[\nc_0W_{j-1}\\le W_j\\le2W_{j-1},\\qquad\n|TW_j|\\le K_*k_jW_j\n\\quad(1\\le j\\le n).\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nR=\\frac2{c_0},\\qquad \\beta=\\log_2R.\n\\]\nFor every \\(p\\in S\\), the function \\(S_p(\\theta)=W_n(e^{i\\theta}p)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nS_p(\\theta+u)\n\\le e^{K_*}R(1+k_n|u|)^\\beta S_p(\\theta),\n\\qquad |u|\\le\\pi.\n\\]\nThe constants in this bound are independent of \\(Q,n,p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the largest \\(1\\le l\\le n\\) such that \\(k_l|u|\\le1\\), or put \\(l=0\\) if no such index exists. The comparisons between successive densities give\n\\[\n\\frac{W_n(e^{i(\\theta+u)}p)}{W_n(e^{i\\theta}p)}\n\\le R^{n-l}\n\\frac{W_l(e^{i(\\theta+u)}p)}{W_l(e^{i\\theta}p)}.\n\\]\nWhen \\(l\\ge1\\), logarithmic differentiation and the phase derivative bound show that the last ratio is at most\n\\[\n\\exp(K_*k_l|u|)\\le e^{K_*}.\n\\]\nFor \\(l=0\\) it equals one. If \\(l=n\\), this proves the assertion. Otherwise maximality, including the case \\(l=0\\), gives\n\\[\nk_n|u|&gt;Q^{n-l-1}\\ge2^{n-l-1}.\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\nR^{n-l}\\le R(1+k_n|u|)^\\beta,\n\\]\nproving the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(A\\ge1\\) and \\(\\beta\\ge0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(A\\ge1\\) and \\(\\beta\\ge0\\). Let \\(q\\ge1\\) be an integer, and let \\(s\\) be a positive trigonometric polynomial of degree at most \\(2q\\). Suppose\n\\[\ns(\\theta+u)\\le A(1+q|u|)^\\beta s(\\theta)\n\\qquad(\\theta\\in\\mathbb R,\\ |u|\\le\\pi).\n\\]\nThere is a constant \\(C(A,\\beta)\\), independent of \\(q,s\\), such that\n\\[\n|s''(\\theta)|\\le C(A,\\beta)q^2s(\\theta).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a fixed smooth compactly supported function \\(\\chi:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R\\) equal to one on \\([-2,2]\\), and put\n\\[\nK_q(u)=\\sum_{h\\in\\mathbb Z}\\chi(h/q)e^{ihu}.\n\\]\nFor normalized convolution on the circle, the frequency assumption implies\n\\[\ns''=s*K_q''.\n\\]\nFor every positive integer \\(N\\),\n\\[\n|K_q''(u)|\\le C_Nq^3(1+q|u|)^{-N},\n\\qquad |u|\\le\\pi,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_N\\) depends only on \\(N,\\chi\\).</p><p>Indeed, the coefficients of \\(K_q''\\) are\n\\[\na_h=-h^2\\chi(h/q)=q^2g(h/q),\\qquad g(v)=-v^2\\chi(v).\n\\]\nThere are \\(O(q)\\) nonzero coefficients, each of size \\(O(q^2)\\), so direct summation gives \\(|K_q''|\\le Cq^3\\). An \\(N\\)-th finite difference of \\(a_h\\) is an iterated integral of the \\(N\\)-th derivative of \\(q^2g(v/q)\\), and hence has size at most \\(C_Nq^{2-N}\\). Only \\(O_N(q)\\) such differences can be nonzero, since \\(g\\) is compactly supported and \\(q\\ge1\\). Their absolute sum is at most \\(C_Nq^{3-N}\\).</p><p>Multiplication of the Fourier series by \\((1-e^{iu})^N\\) produces these differences, up to an index shift. Using\n\\[\n|1-e^{iu}|\\ge2|u|/\\pi\\qquad(-\\pi\\le u\\le\\pi)\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n|K_q''(u)|\\le C_Nq^{3-N}|u|^{-N}\\qquad(u\\ne0).\n\\]\nCombining this with the direct estimate proves the claimed kernel bound.</p><p>Choose an integer \\(N&gt;\\beta+1\\). Positivity and the assumed growth bound now give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|s''(\\theta)|\n&amp;\\le\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}s(\\theta-u)|K_q''(u)|\\,\\frac{du}{2\\pi}\\\\\n&amp;\\le AC_Nq^3s(\\theta)\n\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}(1+q|u|)^{\\beta-N}\\,\\frac{du}{2\\pi}\\\\\n&amp;\\le C(A,\\beta)q^2s(\\theta).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 4.6, including the geometric bounds used immediately before it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a degree-\\(k\\) patch, let \\(A_{j,p}\\) be the correction in Proposition 2.", "statement": "<p>On a degree-\\(k\\) patch, let \\(A_{j,p}\\) be the correction in Proposition 2. Put\n\\[\nF=\\|f\\|_\\infty,\\quad B=\\|b\\|_\\infty,\\quad\nF_1=\\|f'\\|_\\infty,\\quad B_1=\\|b'\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nThere is a constant \\(D_R\\ge1\\), depending only on \\(R_0\\), such that at every sufficiently large degree,\n\\[\n|Yy|\\le D_R,\\qquad |Y\\theta|\\le D_R/k,\\qquad\n|Y(e^{ik\\theta})|\\le D_R,\n\\]\nfor \\(Y=Z/\\sqrt{k}\\) or \\(Y=T/k\\). The horizontal radial arc from \\(e^{i\\theta}p\\) to a patch point has length at most \\(D_R/\\sqrt{k}\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(W_{j-1}&gt;0\\), \\(S_p&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{e^{2y/k}S_p}{W_{j-1}}\\le2,\\qquad\nr_1=\\frac{|S_p'|}{kS_p}\\le1,\\qquad\nr_2=\\frac{|S_p''|}{k^2S_p}\\le1.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|YA_{j,p}|\\le C_2W_{j-1},\n\\qquad\nC_2=2D_R\\bigl[4(F+B)+F_1+B_1\\bigr].\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(C_2\\) is independent of \\(K_*,Q,j,p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>With \\(c=\\langle\\xi,p\\rangle\\), complex horizontality gives\n\\[\n|Zc|\\le\\sqrt{1-|c|^2}\\le\\sqrt{2R_0/k},\n\\qquad |c|\\ge e^{-R_0/k}.\n\\]\nDifferentiate \\(y=-k\\log|c|\\) and \\(\\theta=\\arg c\\) to obtain the first two bounds for \\(Z/\\sqrt{k}\\); the third follows from the second. For \\(T/k\\), one has \\(Yy=0\\) and \\(Y\\theta=1/k\\). The fixed lower threshold for \\(k\\) makes \\(e^{-R_0/k}\\ge1/2\\).</p><p>Choose \\(D_R\\) also large enough to bound the radial arc length. In adapted coordinates this arc is\n\\[\nu\\longmapsto(e^{i\\theta}\\cos u,e^{i\\gamma}\\sin u),\n\\qquad 0\\le u\\le\\arccos(e^{-y/k}).\n\\]\nIt has unit speed and is complex-horizontal. The length bound follows from \\(0\\le y\\le R_0\\) and \\(1-e^{-y/k}\\le R_0/k\\).</p><p>Besides these geometric bounds, use\n\\[\n|Y(e^{2y/k})|\\le(2D_R/k)e^{2y/k},\n\\]\n\\[\n|YS_p|\\le(D_R/k)|S_p'|,\\qquad\n|YS_p'|\\le(D_R/k)|S_p''|.\n\\]\nDifferentiating the correction and taking absolute values gives\n\\[\n|YA_{j,p}|\n\\le D_Re^{2y/k}S_p\n\\bigl[(1+2/k)(F+Br_1)+F_1+B_1r_1+Fr_1+Br_2\\bigr].\n\\]\nSince \\(k\\ge1\\), the assumed normalized bounds imply the stated estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, with the regularized tests and parameter choices of Section 5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One can first choose \\(\\epsilon>0\\), \\(\\eta\\in(0,1/2)\\), and \\(D_*\\ge1\\), depending only on the profile data, so that for every \\(D_0\\ge D_*\\) there are \\(k_*>0\\), \\(d_2>0\\), and an integer threshold \\(Q_*\\) with the …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(a,\\alpha,s_1,f,b,R_0\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a constant \\(C_P\\) such that\n\\[\n|P_k(z)|\\le C_P|z|^k.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), define\n\\[\nF_\\epsilon(v)=\n\\log\\frac{|1-av|^2+\\epsilon^2}{1+\\epsilon^2},\n\\qquad U_k(z)=F_\\epsilon(P_k(z)).\n\\]\nThen \\(F_\\epsilon(0)=U_k(0)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Densities \\(W_j\\), degrees \\(k_j=Q^j\\), and separated peaks are as in Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can first choose \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), \\(\\eta\\in(0,1/2)\\), and \\(D_*\\ge1\\), depending only on the profile data, so that for every \\(D_0\\ge D_*\\) there are \\(k_*&gt;0\\), \\(d_2&gt;0\\), and an integer threshold \\(Q_*\\) with the following property.</p><p>For every integer \\(Q\\ge Q_*\\), all density and degree conditions hold, and, with \\(d_3=d_2/2\\),\n\\[\n\\int_SU_{k_j}(r\\xi)W_N(\\xi)\\,d\\sigma(\\xi)\\le-d_3\n\\]\nwhenever\n\\[\nN\\ge j\\ge1,\\qquad 0\\le r\\le1,\\qquad\n1-\\eta\\le r^{k_j}\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>Each \\(U_k\\) is smooth and plurisubharmonic on \\(\\mathbb C^2\\). For fixed \\(\\epsilon\\) and \\(A&lt;\\infty\\), all derivatives of \\(F_\\epsilon\\) of any fixed order are bounded on \\(|v|\\le A\\), and\n\\[\n|F_\\epsilon(v)|\\le C_{\\epsilon,A}|v|\n\\qquad(|v|\\le A).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The functions \\(U_k\\) are smooth and plurisubharmonic because\n\\[\n(U_k)_{i\\bar j}\n=\\frac{a^2\\epsilon^2}\n{(|1-aP_k|^2+\\epsilon^2)^2}\n(P_k)_i\\overline{(P_k)_j}.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\epsilon\\), the derivative and mean-value bounds for \\(F_\\epsilon\\) follow on each bounded disk. Choose \\(\\epsilon,\\eta,D_0,k_*,d_2\\) as in Lemma 3.2, and put \\(d_3=d_2/2\\).</p><p>Include the density and degree thresholds in \\(Q_*\\), and require \\(Q\\ge4\\). By Proposition 2, the increment \\(W_\\nu-W_{\\nu-1}\\) has frequencies within \\(\\ell_{\\nu-1}\\) of \\(\\pm k_\\nu\\). Since \\(\\ell_{\\nu-1}\\le2k_{\\nu-1}\\), each has absolute value at least\n\\[\n(Q-2)k_{\\nu-1}.\n\\]\nSumming increments with \\(\\nu&gt;j\\) shows that every frequency of \\(W_N-W_j\\) has absolute value at least \\((Q-2)k_j\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(\\xi,r\\), write\n\\[\nV(t')=\\log\\frac{|1-Ae^{it'}|^2+\\epsilon^2}{1+\\epsilon^2},\n\\qquad A=ar^{k_j}P_{k_j}(\\xi).\n\\]\nSince \\(|A|\\le aC_P\\), differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\|V''\\|_\\infty\\le\nB_\\epsilon:=\\frac{2aC_P}{\\epsilon^2}\n+\\frac{4a^2C_P^2}{\\epsilon^4}.\n\\]\nTwo integrations by parts bound its \\(m\\)-th Fourier coefficient by \\(B_\\epsilon/m^2\\) for \\(m\\ne0\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nc_m(\\xi)=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\nF_\\epsilon(r^{k_j}P_{k_j}(\\xi)e^{it'})\ne^{-imt'}\\,\\frac{dt'}{2\\pi}.\n\\]\nHomogeneity and a change of variable give\n\\[\nc_m(e^{i\\omega}\\xi)=e^{imk_j\\omega}c_m(\\xi).\n\\]\nThus \\(c_m\\) has phase frequency \\(mk_j\\), and\n\\[\nU_{k_j}(r\\xi)=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb Z}c_m(\\xi).\n\\]\nThe coefficient bound makes this series uniformly convergent.</p><p>Its truncation to \\(|m|\\le Q-3\\) has uniform error at most \\(2B_\\epsilon/(Q-3)\\), and only frequencies of absolute value less than \\((Q-2)k_j\\). Phase averaging makes its integral against \\(W_N-W_j\\) vanish. Both densities are positive probabilities, so\n\\[\n\\|W_N-W_j\\|_{L^1(\\sigma)}\\le2.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\left|\\int_SU_{k_j}(r\\xi)(W_N-W_j)\\,d\\sigma\\right|\n\\le\\frac{4B_\\epsilon}{Q-3}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(Q_*\\) so this is at most \\(d_2/2\\), and apply Lemma 3.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(k=k_j=Q^j\\), where \\(Q\\) satisfies Proposition 2, and let \\(U_k\\) be defined using any fixed \\(\\epsilon>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(k=k_j=Q^j\\), where \\(Q\\) satisfies Proposition 2, and let \\(U_k\\) be defined using any fixed \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\). For \\(0\\le r\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\int_SU_k(r\\xi)W_j(\\xi)\\,d\\sigma\n=\n\\int_SU_k(r\\xi)\\,d\\sigma\n+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P_k}\n\\int_{U_{k,p}}U_k(r\\xi)e^{2y/k}f(y)\\cos(k\\theta)\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Under global phase rotation, homogeneity gives\n\\[\nP_k(e^{i\\omega}\\xi)=e^{ik\\omega}P_k(\\xi).\n\\]\nThus \\(U_k(r\\xi)\\) has only phase frequencies in \\(k\\mathbb Z\\). By Proposition 2, the phase bandwidth of \\(W_{j-1}\\) is \\(\\ell_{j-1}&lt;k\\), and its constant coefficient on each orbit is one. Averaging the product over the phase therefore gives\n\\[\n\\int_SU_k(r\\xi)W_{j-1}(\\xi)\\,d\\sigma\n=\\int_SU_k(r\\xi)\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]</p><p>Each patch is phase-invariant. Its radial variable \\(y\\) is fixed under rotation, while \\(\\theta,\\gamma\\) both increase by \\(\\omega\\). Write\n\\[\nS_p(\\theta)=\\sum_s\\widehat S_p(s)e^{is\\theta}.\n\\]\nIn the patch increment, the frequencies are \\(\\pm(k+s)\\), with \\(|s|\\le\\ell_{j-1}&lt;k\\). Pairing with a frequency in \\(k\\mathbb Z\\) is possible only for \\(s=0\\). Since \\(\\widehat S_p(0)=1\\) and \\(S_p'\\) has zero constant coefficient, the surviving part of the increment is exactly\n\\[\ne^{2y/k}f(y)\\cos(k\\theta).\n\\]\nIntegrating this phase average proves the identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Proposition 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There exist \\(\\epsilon>0\\), \\(\\eta\\in(0,1/2)\\), and \\(D_*\\ge1\\), depending only on the fixed profile data, such that for every \\(D_0\\ge D_*\\) there are \\(k_*>0\\) and \\(d_2>0\\) with the following property.", "statement": "<p>There exist \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), \\(\\eta\\in(0,1/2)\\), and \\(D_*\\ge1\\), depending only on the fixed profile data, such that for every \\(D_0\\ge D_*\\) there are \\(k_*&gt;0\\) and \\(d_2&gt;0\\) with the following property.</p><p>For every integer \\(Q\\) satisfying Proposition 2 and \\(Q\\ge k_*\\),\n\\[\n\\int_SU_{k_j}(r\\xi)W_j(\\xi)\\,d\\sigma\\le-d_2\n\\]\nfor\n\\[\nj\\ge1,\\qquad 0\\le r\\le1,\\qquad 1-\\eta\\le r^{k_j}\\le1.\n\\]\nThe constants \\(k_*,d_2\\) may depend on \\(D_0\\), but are independent of \\(j\\) and of further increases in \\(Q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\Delta=2s_1(\\cosh\\alpha-1)-\\alpha^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor a single peak define\n\\[\nI_\\epsilon(\\rho)=\n\\int_0^{R_0}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\n\\log\\frac{|1-a\\rho e^{-y}e^{it'}|^2+\\epsilon^2}{1+\\epsilon^2}\n(1+f(y)\\cos t')\\,\\frac{dt'}{2\\pi}\\,dy.\n\\]</p><p>At \\(\\rho=1\\), the unregularized logarithm has only one singularity, at \\((y,t')=(\\alpha,0)\\) modulo \\(2\\pi\\). Near that point,\n\\[\n|1-e^{\\alpha-y+it'}|^2\\asymp(y-\\alpha)^2+(t')^2.\n\\]\nIts logarithm is locally integrable. For \\(0&lt;\\epsilon\\le1\\), the regularized logarithm is bounded above uniformly, and its negative part is bounded by the negative part of the unregularized logarithm plus \\(\\log2\\). Dominated convergence therefore applies.</p><p>For \\(q&lt;1\\), the mean and cosine moment of\n\\(\\log|1-qe^{it'}|^2\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(-q\\), respectively, by the logarithm series. For \\(q&gt;1\\), factoring out \\(q^2\\) gives mean \\(2\\log q\\) and cosine moment \\(-q^{-1}\\). Using the weighted cancellation of \\(f\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_0(1)\n&amp;=\\alpha^2-\\int_0^\\alpha f(y)e^{y-\\alpha}\\,dy\n-\\int_\\alpha^{R_0}f(y)e^{\\alpha-y}\\,dy\\\\\n&amp;=\\alpha^2+\\int_0^\\alpha\nf(y)\\bigl(e^{\\alpha-y}-e^{-(\\alpha-y)}\\bigr)\\,dy\\\\\n&amp;=\\alpha^2-2s_1(\\cosh\\alpha-1)=-\\Delta.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last equality uses \\(f=-s_1\\) on \\([0,\\alpha]\\).</p><p>For \\(k=k_j\\), put \\(\\rho=r^k\\). Homogeneity gives\n\\[\nU_k(r\\xi)=F_\\epsilon(\\rho P_k(\\xi)).\n\\]\nOff the patches, \\(|aP_k|\\le1/2\\). If \\(v=\\rho P_k(\\xi)\\), \\(0\\le\\rho\\le1\\), and \\(s=|1-av|^2\\), then \\(1/4\\le s\\le9/4\\), and\n\\[\nF_\\epsilon(v)-\\log|1-av|^2\n=\\log(1+\\epsilon^2/s)-\\log(1+\\epsilon^2).\n\\]\nThe derivative in \\(s\\) of the first term has magnitude at most \\(16\\epsilon^2\\), and \\(|s-1|\\le C|v|\\). Hence\n\\[\n|F_\\epsilon(v)-\\log|1-av|^2|\n\\le C\\epsilon^2|P_k(\\xi)|.\n\\]\nThe off-patch set is phase-invariant, and the phase mean of the unregularized logarithm is zero. By Proposition 1, its total contribution is at most\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{off}}\\epsilon^2\\,\\frac{N_k}{k},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_{\\mathrm{off}}\\) is independent of \\(D_0,k,\\rho\\) and \\(0&lt;\\epsilon\\le1\\).</p><p>On a patch, Lemma 3.1 and the patch measure formula write the combined contribution as\n\\[\n\\frac2k\\int_0^{R_0}\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}\nF_\\epsilon(\\rho P_k(\\xi))\n\\bigl(e^{-2y/k}+f(y)\\cos(k\\theta)\\bigr)\n\\,\\frac{d\\theta\\,d\\gamma}{(2\\pi)^2}\\,dy.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\epsilon,R_0\\), replace \\(P_k(\\xi)\\) by \\(e^{-y}e^{ik\\theta}\\) using Proposition 1, and replace \\(e^{-2y/k}\\) by one. The bounded derivative of \\(F_\\epsilon\\) and\n\\[\n|e^{-2y/k}-1|\\le2R_0/k\n\\]\nshow that the result differs from \\((2/k)I_\\epsilon(\\rho)\\) by at most\n\\[\n\\frac2k C_{\\epsilon,R_0}\n\\left(e^{-c(D_0-\\sqrt{2R_0})^2}+\\frac1k\\right).\n\\]\nThe constants are uniform over patches and \\(0\\le\\rho\\le1\\). The angular change from \\(k\\theta\\) to \\(t'\\) is exact because \\(k\\) is an integer.</p><p>Choose \\(\\epsilon\\in(0,1]\\) so small that\n\\[\n|I_\\epsilon(1)+\\Delta|\\le\\Delta/8,\\qquad\nC_{\\mathrm{off}}\\epsilon^2\\le\\Delta/8.\n\\]\nBy continuity in \\(\\rho\\), choose \\(\\eta\\in(0,1/2)\\) such that\n\\[\nI_\\epsilon(\\rho)\\le-3\\Delta/4\n\\qquad(1-\\eta\\le\\rho\\le1).\n\\]\nNext choose \\(D_*\\) large enough for patch disjointness and for the first patch error to be at most \\(\\Delta/16\\) inside its factor \\(2/k\\). For each \\(D_0\\ge D_*\\), choose \\(k_*\\) large enough for all geometric estimates and for the remaining error inside that factor to be at most \\(\\Delta/16\\).</p><p>Summing over patches and adding the off-patch estimate gives, in particular,\n\\[\n\\int_SU_k(r\\xi)W_j\\,d\\sigma\n\\le-\\frac\\Delta2\\frac{N_k}{k}\n\\le-\\frac{\\Delta}{2D_0^2}.\n\\]\nThus \\(d_2=\\Delta/(2D_0^2)\\) is a valid choice.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "a controlled potential", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb B,\\mathbb R)\\) and a finite constant \\(C\\) such that, in every centered chart, \\( \\operatorname{Id}\\le h^T(0)\\le C\\operatorname{Id}, \\) and …", "statement": "<p>Definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\n\\mathbb B=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z|&lt;1\\},\\qquad\n\\psi(z)=-\\log(1-|z|^2),\\qquad\nI=(\\psi_{i\\bar j}).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z_0\\in\\mathbb B\\), set \\(r=|z_0|\\), choose a unitary map \\(U\\) with \\(U(r,0)=z_0\\), and define the centered ball chart\n\\[\nT=U\\circ T_r,\\qquad\nT_r(\\zeta)=\n\\left(\n\\frac{r+\\zeta_1}{1+r\\zeta_1},\n\\frac{\\sqrt{1-r^2}\\,\\zeta_2}{1+r\\zeta_1}\n\\right).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth real function \\(\\phi\\), write \\(h=\\partial\\bar\\partial\\phi\\) and\n\\[\nh^T_{i\\bar j}(\\zeta)\n=\\partial_{\\zeta_i}\\partial_{\\bar\\zeta_j}(\\phi\\circ T)(\\zeta).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb B,\\mathbb R)\\) and a finite constant \\(C\\) such that, in every centered chart,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Id}\\le h^T(0)\\le C\\operatorname{Id},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|\\partial_kh^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n+|\\partial_{\\bar l}h^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n+|\\partial_k\\partial_{\\bar l}h^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n\\le C\n\\qquad(i,j,k,l\\in\\{1,2\\}).\n\\]\nEquivalently, the form bounds are \\(I\\le h\\le CI\\).</p><p>There are no \\(H\\in\\mathcal O(\\mathbb B)\\) and \\(C_H\\in\\mathbb R\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}H(z)\n\\le C_H+4\\log\\frac1{1-|z|}+\\phi(z)\n\\qquad(z\\in\\mathbb B).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(a,\\alpha,s_1,f,b,R_0\\) as in Proposition 1. Lemma 3.2 first chooses \\(\\epsilon,\\eta\\) and then permits any sufficiently large spacing \\(D_0\\). Fix such a \\(D_0\\), its degree threshold \\(k_*\\), and \\(d_3=d_2/2&gt;0\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 holds above a threshold depending only on the fixed patch data and spacing, with \\(K_*\\) fixed before that threshold. Choose one integer \\(Q\\) satisfying this density threshold, \\(Q\\ge k_*\\), and the Fourier-error threshold in Proposition 3.</p><p>Fix a finite \\(L&gt;5\\log Q/d_3\\). Lemma 4.2 constructs the smooth potential with \\(h\\ge I\\); Lemma 4.3 proves the required obstruction; and Lemma 4.4 supplies the finite uniform chart bounds.</p><p>The chart constant may depend on all these fixed choices, including \\(L\\). The metric parameter used later is chosen only afterward from these bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "representing-mean criterion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(u\\) be a continuous real function on \\(\\mathbb B\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(u\\) be a continuous real function on \\(\\mathbb B\\). Suppose there are radii \\(0&lt;r_N&lt;1\\) and probabilities \\(\\mu_N\\) on \\(S\\), representing holomorphic evaluation at zero, such that\n\\[\n\\int_Su(r_N\\xi)\\,d\\mu_N(\\xi)\\longrightarrow-\\infty.\n\\]\nThen there are no \\(H\\in\\mathcal O(\\mathbb B)\\) and \\(C_H\\in\\mathbb R\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}H\\le C_H+u\\quad\\text{on }\\mathbb B.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For each fixed \\(r_N&lt;1\\), the Taylor polynomials of \\(H\\) converge uniformly on \\(r_NS\\). The representing identities therefore give\n\\[\n\\int_S\\operatorname{Re}H(r_N\\xi)\\,d\\mu_N(\\xi)\n=\\operatorname{Re}H(0).\n\\]\nIntegrating the proposed inequality contradicts the assumed limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix the parameters and an integer \\(Q\\) for which Propositions 2 and 3 hold, and set \\(k_j=Q^j\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix the parameters and an integer \\(Q\\) for which Propositions 2 and 3 hold, and set \\(k_j=Q^j\\). For every finite \\(L&gt;0\\), the series\n\\[\n\\phi(z)=\\psi(z)+L\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty U_{k_j}(z)\n\\]\nconverges locally in \\(C^\\infty(\\mathbb B)\\). Its sum is smooth, satisfies \\(\\phi(0)=0\\), and has complex Hessian \\(h\\ge I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;r_0&lt;r_1&lt;1\\). Around each point of \\(|z|\\le r_0\\), the complex polydisk of radius\n\\[\n(r_1-r_0)/(2\\sqrt2)\n\\]\nlies in \\(|z|&lt;r_1\\). Cauchy estimates there, together with Proposition 1, give\n\\[\n\\sup_{|z|\\le r_0}|D^mP_k(z)|\n\\le C_{m,r_0,r_1}r_1^k\n\\]\nfor every fixed derivative order \\(m\\). Here \\(D^m\\) denotes any real partial derivative of total order \\(m\\).</p><p>The values of \\(P_k\\) remain in a fixed bounded disk. Every positive-order derivative of \\(F_\\epsilon(P_k)\\) is a sum of products containing at least one positive-order derivative of \\(P_k\\) or its conjugate. The bounded derivatives of \\(F_\\epsilon\\) therefore give the same exponential estimate for \\(D^mU_k\\). For \\(m=0\\), use\n\\[\n|F_\\epsilon(v)|\\le C_{\\epsilon,A}|v|.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sum_jr_1^{Q^j}&lt;\\infty\\), the series converges in every local \\(C^m\\) norm.</p><p>Each summand vanishes at zero and is plurisubharmonic by Proposition 3. Local \\(C^2\\) convergence justifies termwise differentiation, and hence\n\\[\n\\partial\\bar\\partial\\phi\n=I+L\\sum_j\\partial\\bar\\partial U_{k_j}\\ge I.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the potential of Lemma 4.2 and the constant \\(d_3>0\\) from Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>Use the potential of Lemma 4.2 and the constant \\(d_3&gt;0\\) from Proposition 3. If\n\\[\nL&gt;\\frac{5\\log Q}{d_3},\n\\]\nthen there are no \\(H\\in\\mathcal O(\\mathbb B)\\) and \\(C_H\\in\\mathbb R\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}H(z)\n\\le C_H+4\\log\\frac1{1-|z|}+\\phi(z).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nr_N=1-\\frac{\\eta}{k_N}.\n\\]\nFor \\(j\\le N\\), Bernoulli’s inequality gives\n\\[\nr_N^{k_j}\n=\\left(1-\\frac{\\eta}{k_N}\\right)^{k_j}\n\\ge1-\\eta\\frac{k_j}{k_N}\\ge1-\\eta.\n\\]\nThus the first \\(N\\) modes satisfy the hypothesis of Proposition 3 at this same radius.</p><p>For \\(j=N+m&gt;N\\),\n\\[\nr_N^{k_j}\\le e^{-\\eta k_j/k_N}=e^{-\\eta Q^m}.\n\\]\nBy the uniform polynomial bound and the mean-value estimate for \\(F_\\epsilon\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j&gt;N}\\sup_{\\xi\\in S}|U_{k_j}(r_N\\xi)|\n\\le C_\\epsilon\\sum_{m\\ge1}e^{-\\eta Q^m}\n=:C_{\\mathrm{tail}}&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nindependently of \\(N\\). Because \\(W_N\\) is a positive probability, the same bound controls the weighted tail, without a uniform pointwise bound on \\(W_N\\).</p><p>The base potential contributes only one logarithm:\n\\[\n\\psi(r_N\\xi)\n=-\\log(1-r_N^2)\n\\le\\log\\frac1{1-r_N}\n=\\log(k_N/\\eta).\n\\]\nCombining these estimates with Proposition 3,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\int_S\\left(\\phi(r_N\\xi)+4\\log\\frac1{1-r_N}\\right)W_N\\,d\\sigma\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\le5\\log(k_N/\\eta)+L(-d_3N+C_{\\mathrm{tail}})\\\\\n&amp;\\quad=N(5\\log Q-Ld_3)\n+5\\log(1/\\eta)+LC_{\\mathrm{tail}}\n\\longrightarrow-\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBy Proposition 2, \\(\\mu_N=W_N\\,d\\sigma\\) are positive representing probabilities. Apply Lemma 4.1 with\n\\[\nu(z)=\\phi(z)+4\\log\\frac1{1-|z|}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed finite \\(L>0\\), the potential in Lemma 4.2 has uniformly bounded positive-order derivatives through order four at the origins of all centered ball charts.", "statement": "<p>For every fixed finite \\(L&gt;0\\), the potential in Lemma 4.2 has uniformly bounded positive-order derivatives through order four at the origins of all centered ball charts.</p><p>Consequently, its complex Hessian satisfies all the bounds stated in Proposition 4, with lower constant \\(1\\) and a finite upper constant \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>After a unitary change of coordinates, put the center at \\((r,0)\\), \\(0\\le r&lt;1\\), and let \\(\\delta=1-r\\). Rotating \\(P_k\\) preserves its degree and uniform homogeneous bound. Use \\(T_r\\) on the fixed complex polydisk\n\\[\nD_\\rho=\\{|\\zeta_1|&lt;\\rho,\\ |\\zeta_2|&lt;\\rho\\},\n\\qquad \\rho=\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nThe denominators in \\(T_r\\) have modulus at least \\(1-\\rho\\).</p><p>For high degrees \\(k\\delta&gt;1\\), the ball automorphism identity gives\n\\[\n1-|T_r(\\zeta)|^2\n=\\frac{(1-r^2)(1-|\\zeta|^2)}{|1+r\\zeta_1|^2}\n\\ge\\frac{1-2\\rho^2}{(1+\\rho)^2}\\delta.\n\\]\nThus \\(1-|T_r(\\zeta)|\\ge c\\delta\\), and\n\\[\n|P_k(T_r(\\zeta))|\\le C_Pe^{-ck\\delta}.\n\\]\nCauchy estimates in this fixed polydisk bound every fixed-order holomorphic derivative at zero, including order zero, by \\(C_me^{-ck\\delta}\\).</p><p>For low degrees \\(k\\delta\\le1\\), consider\n\\[\nA_k=\n\\left\\{|z_1-r|&lt;\\frac1k,\\quad |z_2|&lt;\\frac1{\\sqrt{k}}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIt may extend outside the ball, but \\(P_k\\) is entire and homogeneous, so Proposition 1 applies there. In fact,\n\\[\n|z|^2\\le(1+1/k)^2+1/k\\le1+4/k,\n\\]\n\\[\n|P_k(z)|\\le C_P(1+2/k)^k\\le C_Pe^2.\n\\]\nCauchy estimates on the concentric half-polydisk give\n\\[\n|\\partial_1P_k|\\le Ck,\\qquad\n|\\partial_2P_k|\\le C\\sqrt{k}.\n\\]\nMeanwhile,\n\\[\n|T_r^1(\\zeta)-r|\\le\\frac{2\\rho}{1-\\rho}\\delta,\n\\qquad\n|T_r^2(\\zeta)|\\le\\frac{\\sqrt2\\,\\rho}{1-\\rho}\\sqrt{\\delta}.\n\\]\nBoth numerical coefficients are less than \\(1/2\\). As \\(\\delta\\le1/k\\), the chart image lies in the half-polydisk above. Integrating the gradient along the straight segment from \\((r,0)\\) to \\(T_r(\\zeta)\\) gives\n\\[\n|P_k(T_r(\\zeta))-P_k(r,0)|\n\\le C(k\\delta+\\sqrt{k\\delta})\n\\le2C\\sqrt{k\\delta}.\n\\]\nThe segment stays in the half-polydisk by convexity. Applying Cauchy estimates on \\(D_\\rho\\) to the holomorphic difference shows that every positive-order derivative at zero is bounded by \\(C_m\\sqrt{k\\delta}\\). No such assertion is made for the zeroth-order value in this range.</p><p>In both ranges the values of \\(P_k\\circ T_r\\) remain in a fixed bounded disk. Each positive-order real derivative of\n\\[\nU_k\\circ T_r=F_\\epsilon(P_k\\circ T_r)\n\\]\nis a sum of products containing differentiated factors of \\(P_k\\circ T_r\\) or its conjugate. For \\(1\\le m\\le4\\),\n\\[\n|D^m(U_k\\circ T_r)(0)|\n\\le C_{\\epsilon,m}\n\\begin{cases}\n\\sqrt{k\\delta},&amp;k\\delta\\le1,\\\\\ne^{-ck\\delta},&amp;k\\delta&gt;1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nEvery term contains at least one differentiated factor; products obey the same bound because the displayed scale factor is at most one.</p><p>Let \\(J\\) be the largest index with \\(Q^J\\delta\\le1\\), or put \\(J=0\\) if none exists. Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\le J}\\sqrt{Q^j\\delta}\n\\le\\frac1{1-Q^{-1/2}}.\n\\]\nFor the remaining degrees, the first \\(Q^j\\delta\\) exceeds one, so\n\\[\n\\sum_{j&gt;J}e^{-cQ^j\\delta}\n\\le\\sum_{m\\ge0}e^{-cQ^m}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nBoth bounds are uniform in the center. Termwise differentiation is legitimate by Lemma 4.2, and multiplication by the fixed amplitude \\(L\\) preserves these bounds.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\psi\\circ T_r\n=-\\log(1-|\\zeta|^2)-\\log(1-r^2)\n+\\log|1+r\\zeta_1|^2.\n\\]\nIts positive-order derivatives through order four at zero are uniformly bounded. The last term is pluriharmonic, so its complex Hessian is exactly the standard ball Hessian in these coordinates. The required Hessian component bounds use derivatives of orders two, three, and four. Together with \\(h\\ge I\\), this proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "the Jacobian on the zero section", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb B,\\mathbb R)\\) satisfy the following nonexistence property:", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb B,\\mathbb R)\\) satisfy the following nonexistence property: no \\(H\\in\\mathcal O(\\mathbb B)\\) and \\(C_H\\in\\mathbb R\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}H(z)\n\\le C_H+4\\log\\frac1{1-|z|}+\\phi(z).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nM_\\phi=\n\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb B\\times\\mathbb C:\ne^{\\phi(z)}|w|^2&lt;1\\}.\n\\]\nThen no bounded holomorphic map \\(F:M_\\phi\\to\\mathbb C^3\\) has a nowhere-vanishing complex Jacobian determinant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(F=(F_1,F_2,F_3)\\) is such a map, and choose \\(B\\ge1\\) with \\(|F_l|\\le B\\) on \\(M_\\phi\\). Since \\(M_\\phi\\) is open in \\(\\mathbb C^3\\), its coordinates \\((z_1,z_2,w)\\) are global. The function\n\\[\nd(z)=\n\\det\\frac{\\partial(F_1,F_2,F_3)}\n{\\partial(z_1,z_2,w)}(z,0)\n\\]\nis holomorphic and nowhere zero on \\(\\mathbb B\\).</p><p>Fix \\(z\\in\\mathbb B\\), and put \\(\\delta=1-|z|\\). The restriction \\(F_l(\\,\\cdot\\,,0)\\) is bounded and holomorphic on the entire base ball. For \\(j=1,2\\), the disk\n\\[\n\\{z+\\zeta e_j:|\\zeta|&lt;\\delta\\}\n\\]\nis contained in that ball. Cauchy’s estimate, with radii tending to \\(\\delta\\), gives\n\\[\n|\\partial_{z_j}F_l(z,0)|\\le B\\delta^{-1}.\n\\]\nFor this fixed \\(z\\), the fiber is the disk of radius \\(e^{-\\phi(z)/2}\\), so\n\\[\n|\\partial_wF_l(z,0)|\\le Be^{\\phi(z)/2}.\n\\]\nThe base estimate requires no uniform fiber radius near \\(z\\); it is taken entirely on the zero section.</p><p>The six terms of the determinant expansion yield\n\\[\n|d(z)|\\le6B^3\\delta^{-2}e^{\\phi(z)/2},\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\log|d(z)|^2\n\\le2\\log6+6\\log B\n+4\\log\\frac1{1-|z|}+\\phi(z).\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\mathbb B\\) is simply connected and \\(d\\) has no zeros, it has a global holomorphic logarithm \\(\\ell\\). A primitive of the closed holomorphic one-form \\(d^{-1}\\partial d\\), with a suitable constant, satisfies \\(e^\\ell=d\\). The function \\(H=2\\ell\\) has\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}H=\\log|d|^2,\n\\]\ncontradicting the assumed nonexistence property.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "global Hartogs geometry", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\phi\\) be a smooth real plurisubharmonic function on \\(\\mathbb B=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z|<1\\}\\), and let \\(\\lambda>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\phi\\) be a smooth real plurisubharmonic function on\n\\(\\mathbb B=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z|&lt;1\\}\\), and let \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\). Define\n\\[\n\\psi(z)=-\\log(1-|z|^2),\\qquad\nt(z,w)=e^{\\phi(z)}|w|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nM_\\phi=\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb B\\times\\mathbb C:t&lt;1\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_\\lambda=\\lambda\\psi-\\log(1-t),\\qquad\n\\widehat g=\\partial\\bar\\partial\\mathcal K_\\lambda,\n\\qquad g=2\\operatorname{Re}\\widehat g.\n\\]\nThe real-metric convention means that, if a real vector \\(X\\) has \\((1,0)\\) part \\(u\\), then\n\\[\ng(X,X)=2\\widehat g(u,u).\n\\]</p><p>Then \\(M_\\phi\\) is contractible, \\(g\\) is a smooth geodesically complete Kähler metric, and \\(\\mathcal K_\\lambda\\) is a smooth strictly plurisubharmonic exhaustion of \\(M_\\phi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The function \\(t\\) is smooth on \\(\\mathbb B\\times\\mathbb C\\), so \\(M_\\phi=\\{t&lt;1\\}\\) is open. The map\n\\[\n(z,w)\\longmapsto(z,e^{\\phi(z)/2}w)\n\\]\nis a smooth real diffeomorphism from \\(M_\\phi\\) to \\(\\mathbb B\\times\\mathbb D\\), with inverse\n\\[\n(z,u)\\longmapsto(z,e^{-\\phi(z)/2}u).\n\\]\nThus \\(M_\\phi\\) is contractible; no holomorphic trivialization is needed.</p><p>The potential is smooth everywhere, including the zero section. Write\n\\[\nh=\\partial\\bar\\partial\\phi,\\qquad\nx=\\frac{t}{1-t},\\qquad\nG=\\lambda I+xh,\\qquad\n\\vartheta=dw+w\\partial\\phi.\n\\]\nThe base covectors together with \\(\\vartheta\\) form a global smooth complex coframe. Differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\widehat g\n=G+\\frac{e^\\phi}{(1-t)^2}\\vartheta\\otimes\\overline\\vartheta.\n\\]\nThus, for a complex tangent vector \\(u=(u_z,u_w)\\),\n\\[\n\\widehat g(u,u)\n=G(u_z,u_z)\n+\\frac{e^\\phi}{(1-t)^2}\n|u_w+w\\partial\\phi(u_z)|^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(h\\ge0\\), \\(G\\ge\\lambda I\\), and the form is positive definite. At the zero section,\n\\[\n\\widehat g|_{(z,0)}\n=\\lambda I(z)+e^{\\phi(z)}dw\\otimes d\\bar w.\n\\]\nBeing the positive complex Hessian of a smooth real potential, it defines a Kähler metric.</p><p>Away from the zero section, write \\(s=\\log t\\) and\n\\[\nq=x(1+x)=\\frac{t}{(1-t)^2}.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\partial s=\\vartheta/w\\), so\n\\[\n\\widehat g=\\lambda I+xh+q\\,\\partial s\\otimes\\overline{\\partial s}.\n\\]</p><p>For a real smooth function \\(f\\), the metric convention gives\n\\[\n|\\nabla f|_g^2=2|\\partial f|_{\\widehat g^{-1}}^2.\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(g(X,X)=2\\widehat g(u,u)\\) and \\(df(X)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\partial f(u)\\), and the identity follows by taking dual norms.</p><p>Globally,\n\\[\n\\partial[-\\log(1-t)]\n=\\frac{e^\\phi\\bar w}{1-t}\\vartheta.\n\\]\nThe metric is block diagonal in the coframe above. Hence\n\\[\n|\\partial[-\\log(1-t)]|_{\\widehat g^{-1}}^2\n=\\frac{|e^\\phi\\bar w/(1-t)|^2}{e^\\phi/(1-t)^2}=t.\n\\]\nThis includes \\(w=0\\), where both sides are zero.</p><p>Direct inversion of the base Hessian gives\n\\[\n|\\partial\\psi|_{I^{-1}}^2=|z|^2.\n\\]\nThe covector \\(\\partial\\psi\\) has only base components, and \\(G\\ge\\lambda I\\). Therefore\n\\[\n|\\nabla[-\\log(1-t)]|_g^2=2t&lt;2,\\qquad\n|\\nabla\\psi|_g^2\\le\\frac{2|z|^2}{\\lambda}\\le\\frac2\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(p\\in M_\\phi\\) and \\(R\\ge0\\). Integrating these bounds along arbitrary piecewise smooth paths and taking the infimum of their lengths shows that \\(d_g(p,q)\\le R\\) implies\n\\[\n-\\log(1-t(q))\\le a_R:=-\\log(1-t(p))+\\sqrt2R,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\psi(q)\\le b_R:=\\psi(p)+\\sqrt{2/\\lambda}\\,R.\n\\]\nNo minimizing geodesic is required. Put\n\\[\n\\tau_R=1-e^{-a_R}&lt;1,\\qquad\n\\rho_R=\\sqrt{1-e^{-b_R}}&lt;1.\n\\]\nEvery such \\(q=(z,w)\\) satisfies \\(t(q)\\le\\tau_R\\) and \\(|z|\\le\\rho_R\\). If \\(m_R\\) is the minimum of \\(\\phi\\) on \\(\\{|z|\\le\\rho_R\\}\\), then\n\\[\n|w|^2=e^{-\\phi(z)}t(q)\\le e^{-m_R}\\tau_R.\n\\]\nConsequently the metric ball is contained in\n\\[\nK_R=\\{(z,w):|z|\\le\\rho_R,\\ e^{\\phi(z)}|w|^2\\le\\tau_R\\}.\n\\]\nThis set is closed and bounded in \\(\\mathbb C^3\\), hence compact, and lies entirely inside \\(M_\\phi\\). A bounded-length curve therefore cannot escape through the base boundary, a fiber boundary, or unbounded fiber coordinates.</p><p>Both \\(\\psi\\) and \\(-\\log(1-t)\\) are nonnegative. Thus \\(\\mathcal K_\\lambda\\le R\\) implies\n\\[\n\\psi\\le R/\\lambda,\\qquad -\\log(1-t)\\le R.\n\\]\nThese inequalities place each sublevel set in a compact set of the same form as \\(K_R\\); it is closed there, hence compact. Positivity of the complex Hessian proves strict plurisubharmonicity.</p><p>Finally, suppose a unit-speed geodesic has finite maximal forward time \\(T\\). Its image lies in \\(K_T\\). On this compact set, the smooth positive metric has a uniform positive Euclidean lower bound, so Euclidean velocity is bounded. The Christoffel symbols are bounded, and the geodesic equation bounds Euclidean acceleration. Position and velocity have limits as time tends to \\(T\\), with limiting position in \\(K_T\\subset M_\\phi\\). Local existence for the smooth geodesic equation extends the geodesic beyond \\(T\\), a contradiction. Reverse time gives geodesic completeness.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "the full curvature tensor", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Define \\( D=(1+2x)h-qhG^{-1}h. \\) Then \\( R=-\\lambda S_I-qS_h-S_V -(S_{D+V}-S_D-S_V)+T_4+T_3, \\) where \\(T_j\\) has exactly \\(j\\) horizontal indices.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb B,\\mathbb R)\\), and let \\(h=\\partial\\bar\\partial\\phi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In every centered chart of Proposition 4, assume fixed constants \\(0&lt;c\\le C&lt;\\infty\\) satisfy\n\\[\nc\\operatorname{Id}\\le h^T(0)\\le C\\operatorname{Id},\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\partial_kh^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n+|\\partial_{\\bar l}h^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n+|\\partial_k\\partial_{\\bar l}h^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n\\le C.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the Hartogs metric of Proposition 6, with \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\).</p><p>Pointwise definitions:\nAt a point with \\(w\\ne0\\), choose centered base coordinates and a local branch of \\(\\log w\\). Put\n\\[\ns=\\log t,\\qquad x=\\frac{t}{1-t},\\qquad q=x(1+x),\n\\qquad G=\\lambda I+xh.\n\\]\nMake the holomorphic fiber change\n\\[\nP(z)=\\sum_i\\phi_i(0)z_i\n+\\frac12\\sum_{i,k}\\phi_{ik}(0)z_iz_k,\\qquad\nv=\\log w+P(z).\n\\]\nThen \\(s=v+\\bar v+\\widetilde\\phi\\), where\n\\(\\widetilde\\phi=\\phi-P-\\bar P\\), and at the point\n\\[\ns_i=s_{ik}=0,\\qquad s_v=s_{\\bar v}=1,\\qquad s_{i\\bar j}=h_{i\\bar j}.\n\\]\nNormalize the vertical vector to \\(q^{-1/2}\\partial_v\\) and denote its index by \\(0\\). Horizontal indices range over \\(1,2\\). In this frame the metric is \\(G+V\\), where \\(V_{0\\bar0}=1\\) is the only nonzero entry of \\(V\\). Horizontal forms are extended by zero vertically.</p><p>For a Hermitian form \\(P_0\\), define\n\\[\n(S_{P_0})_{a\\bar b c\\bar d}\n=(P_0)_{a\\bar b}(P_0)_{c\\bar d}\n+(P_0)_{a\\bar d}(P_0)_{c\\bar b}.\n\\]\nUse the curvature convention\n\\[\nR_{a\\bar b c\\bar d}\n=-\\mathcal K_{ac\\bar b\\bar d}\n+\\widehat g^{p\\bar e}\\mathcal K_{ac\\bar e}\\mathcal K_{p\\bar b\\bar d}.\n\\]\nThe inverse coefficients satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_p\\widehat g^{p\\bar e}\\widehat g_{p\\bar j}=\\delta_{ej};\n\\]\ntheir numerical array is the transpose of the ordinary matrix inverse.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Define\n\\[\nD=(1+2x)h-qhG^{-1}h.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nR=-\\lambda S_I-qS_h-S_V\n-(S_{D+V}-S_D-S_V)+T_4+T_3,\n\\]\nwhere \\(T_j\\) has exactly \\(j\\) horizontal indices. Its nonzero components, up to Kähler symmetries and conjugation, are\n\\[\n(T_4)_{i\\bar j k\\bar l}\n=-xh_{i\\bar j,k\\bar l}\n+x^2G^{p\\bar e}h_{i\\bar e,k}h_{p\\bar j,\\bar l},\n\\]\n\\[\n(T_3)_{i\\bar j k\\bar0}\n=\\sqrt q\\left(\n-h_{i\\bar j,k}\n+xG^{p\\bar e}h_{i\\bar e,k}h_{p\\bar j}\n\\right).\n\\]\nUniformly in \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\), their Euclidean component norms satisfy\n\\[\n\\|T_4\\|\\le C_4x,\\qquad \\|T_3\\|\\le C_3\\sqrt q,\n\\]\nwith constants depending only on the chart bounds.</p><p>If \\(\\lambda\\) is at least the uniform upper eigenvalue bound for \\(h\\), then\n\\[\n(1+x)h\\le D\\le(1+2x)h.\n\\]</p><p>At the zero section the same tensor formula holds with\n\\[\nx=q=0,\\quad G=\\lambda I,\\quad D=h,\\quad T_3=T_4=0,\n\\]\nusing the regular normalized vertical vector \\(e^{-\\phi/2}\\partial_w\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>All higher derivatives of \\(s\\) involving a vertical index vanish. Subtracting \\(P+\\bar P\\) changes neither \\(h\\) nor its component derivatives. The pointwise metric has blocks \\(\\operatorname{diag}(G,q)\\).</p><p>Write \\(F(s)=-\\log(1-e^s)\\). Then\n\\[\nF'=x,\\qquad F''=q,\\qquad\nF'''=q(1+2x),\\qquad\nF''''=q(1+6x+6x^2).\n\\]\nThe centered expression for \\(\\psi\\) differs from \\(-\\log(1-|z|^2)\\) by a constant and a pluriharmonic function. Its derivatives of type \\((2,1)\\) vanish at the center, and its derivatives of type \\((2,2)\\) are \\(S_I\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nA_{ik\\bar j}=h_{i\\bar j,k},\\qquad\nB_{ik\\bar j\\bar l}=h_{i\\bar j,k\\bar l}.\n\\]\nBefore normalizing the vertical vector, the only possibly nonzero third derivatives of type \\((2,1)\\), up to symmetry, are\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{ik\\bar j}=xA_{ik\\bar j},\\qquad\n\\mathcal K_{iv\\bar j}=qh_{i\\bar j},\\qquad\n\\mathcal K_{vv\\bar v}=q(1+2x).\n\\]\nThe fourth derivatives of type \\((2,2)\\), up to symmetry and conjugation, are\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{ik\\bar j\\bar l}\n=\\lambda(S_I)_{i\\bar j k\\bar l}\n+xB_{ik\\bar j\\bar l}+q(S_h)_{i\\bar j k\\bar l},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{ik\\bar j\\bar v}=qA_{ik\\bar j},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal K_{iv\\bar j\\bar v}=q(1+2x)h_{i\\bar j},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{vv\\bar v\\bar v}=q(1+6x+6x^2).\n\\]\nThese lists follow by the chain rule and the gauge identities in the statement. In the four-horizontal derivative, the two mixed-second-derivative pairings give \\(qS_h\\); the third pairing contains \\(s_{ik}s_{\\bar j\\bar l}=0\\). Other terms contain a vanished first or pure second horizontal derivative. In particular,\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{ik\\bar v}=\\mathcal K_{vv\\bar j}\n=\\mathcal K_{ik\\bar v\\bar v}=0,\n\\]\nand fourth derivatives with one horizontal and three vertical indices vanish. No vanishing of pure third horizontal derivatives is needed.</p><p>Substitution into the curvature convention gives the displayed \\(T_4,T_3\\). The remaining normalized curvature types are\n\\[\nR_{i\\bar j0\\bar0}\n=-(1+2x)h_{i\\bar j}+q(hG^{-1}h)_{i\\bar j}\n=-D_{i\\bar j},\n\\]\n\\[\nR_{i\\bar0k\\bar0}=0,\\qquad\nR_{i\\bar00\\bar0}=0,\\qquad\nR_{0\\bar00\\bar0}=-2.\n\\]\nThe last identity follows because the unnormalized value is\n\\[\n-q(1+6x+6x^2)+q(1+2x)^2=-2q^2,\n\\]\nand the four vertical normalization factors multiply to \\(q^{-2}\\). The tensors \\(S_V\\) and \\(S_{D+V}-S_D-S_V\\) give precisely these vertical and balanced two-horizontal blocks. Kähler symmetries and conjugation account for all components.</p><p>Since \\(h\\ge cI\\),\n\\[\n\\|G^{-1}\\|\\le\\frac1{\\lambda+cx},\n\\qquad\nx^2\\|G^{-1}\\|\\le\\frac xc.\n\\]\nThe component-derivative bounds applied to \\(T_4,T_3\\) give the stated norm estimates.</p><p>At the center, \\(G=\\lambda I+xh\\) commutes with \\(h\\). On an eigendirection of \\(h\\) with eigenvalue \\(d\\), the ratio of \\(D\\) to \\(h\\) is\n\\[\n1+2x-\\frac{x(1+x)d}{\\lambda+xd}.\n\\]\nThe subtracted fraction is nonnegative and is at most \\(x\\) when \\(\\lambda\\ge d\\). This proves the bounds for \\(D\\).</p><p>At the zero section, a holomorphic fiber change \\(W=e^{P(z)+a}w\\), with \\(a\\) constant and \\(P\\) of degree at most two, makes the new weight have value zero and vanishing first and pure second derivatives at the base point. Its mixed Hessian remains \\(h\\). Through total degree four, the terms relevant to curvature are\n\\[\n\\lambda\\psi\n+|W|^2\\left(1+\\sum_{i,j}h_{i\\bar j}z_i\\bar z_j\\right)\n+\\frac12|W|^4.\n\\]\nAt the origin the metric is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda I,1)\\), all relevant third derivatives vanish, and\n\\[\nR_{i\\bar j k\\bar l}=-\\lambda(S_I)_{i\\bar j k\\bar l},\n\\qquad R_{i\\bar j0\\bar0}=-h_{i\\bar j},\n\\qquad R_{0\\bar00\\bar0}=-2,\n\\]\nwith all other types zero. This is the asserted extension.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(g=2\\operatorname{Re}\\widehat g\\) and the curvature convention of Proposition 7, \\( K_g(\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{U_1,U_2\\}) =\\frac{\\mathcal H_R}{2a_{\\widehat g}}, \\qquad a_{\\widehat g}>0. \\) For the tensor …", "statement": "<p>Real-plane curvature identities and error estimates; assembling original Lemmas 8.2–8.4.</p><p>Definitions:</p><p class=\"li\">Use a pointwise frame \\(\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C\\), with horizontal identity form \\(I\\) and vertical form \\(V\\) as in Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U_1,U_2\\) be real linearly independent tangent vectors with \\((1,0)\\) parts \\(u_1,u_2\\), so \\(U_j=u_j+\\bar u_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a Hermitian form \\(P\\), linear in its first argument, define\n\\[\na_P=P(u_1,u_1)P(u_2,u_2)\n-(\\operatorname{Re}P(u_1,u_2))^2,\n\\qquad\nb_P=\\operatorname{Im}P(u_1,u_2).\n\\]\nFor horizontal \\(P\\), define\n\\[\nm_P=a_{P+V}-a_P-a_V.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a tensor \\(T\\) with the Kähler symmetries and Hermitian conjugation symmetry, put\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_T\n=T_{u_1\\bar u_1u_2\\bar u_2}\n-\\operatorname{Re}T_{u_1\\bar u_2u_1\\bar u_2}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\Xi^{a\\bar b}\n=u_1^a\\overline{u_2^b}-u_2^a\\overline{u_1^b}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\Xi_{HH}\\) be its horizontal block and \\(\\Xi_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\) its two mixed blocks, with all other entries zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(g=2\\operatorname{Re}\\widehat g\\) and the curvature convention of Proposition 7,\n\\[\nK_g(\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{U_1,U_2\\})\n=\\frac{\\mathcal H_R}{2a_{\\widehat g}},\n\\qquad a_{\\widehat g}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor the tensor in Proposition 7,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\mathcal H_R\n={}&amp;\\lambda(a_I+3b_I^2)+q(a_h+3b_h^2)\n+4a_V+m_D+6b_Db_V\\\\\n&amp;-\\mathcal H_{T_4}-\\mathcal H_{T_3},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{T_4}|\\le Cxa_I,\\qquad\n|\\mathcal H_{T_3}|\\le C\\sqrt q\\,\\sqrt{a_Im_I}.\n\\]\nMore explicitly,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{T_3}|\n\\le C\\sqrt q\\,\\|\\Xi_{HH}\\|\\,\\|\\Xi_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\|.\n\\]\nThe constants depend only on the tensor bounds in Proposition 7.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 gives the real sectional-curvature quotient and\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{S_I}=a_I+3b_I^2,\\qquad\n\\mathcal H_{S_h}=a_h+3b_h^2.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 8.2, \\(a_V=b_V^2\\), so\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{S_V}=4a_V.\n\\]\nThe same identity gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{S_{D+V}-S_D-S_V}=m_D+6b_Db_V.\n\\]\nApply these identities to the tensor decomposition of Proposition 7. Lemma 8.3 supplies the error estimates, including the sharper mixed-block form.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With the definitions of Proposition 8, \\( K_g(\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{U_1,U_2\\}) =\\frac{\\mathcal H_R}{2a_{\\widehat g}}. \\) For every tensor \\(T\\) with the Kähler symmetries and Hermitian conjugation symmetry …", "statement": "<p>With the definitions of Proposition 8,\n\\[\nK_g(\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{U_1,U_2\\})\n=\\frac{\\mathcal H_R}{2a_{\\widehat g}}.\n\\]\nFor every tensor \\(T\\) with the Kähler symmetries and Hermitian conjugation symmetry,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_T\n=-\\frac12T_{a\\bar b c\\bar d}\n\\Xi^{a\\bar b}\\Xi^{c\\bar d}.\n\\]\nFor every Hermitian form \\(P\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{S_P}=a_P+3b_P^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The real squared area is \\(4a_{\\widehat g}\\), because\n\\[\ng(U_i,U_j)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\widehat g(u_i,u_j).\n\\]\nIn particular \\(a_{\\widehat g}&gt;0\\). Write\n\\[\nA=R_{u_1\\bar u_1u_2\\bar u_2},\n\\qquad B=R_{u_1\\bar u_2u_1\\bar u_2}.\n\\]\nThe Kähler symmetries in the expansion on \\(U_j=u_j+\\bar u_j\\) give\n\\[\nR^{\\mathbb R}(U_1,U_2,U_2,U_1)\n=A-B-\\bar B+A=2\\mathcal H_R.\n\\]\nDivision by the real squared area proves the sectional-curvature formula.</p><p>Expanding the contraction with \\(\\Xi\\) gives the same expression \\(A-\\operatorname{Re}B\\) for \\(T\\). Finally, if \\(c_P=P(u_1,u_2)\\), direct contraction gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal H_{S_P}\n&amp;=P(u_1,u_1)P(u_2,u_2)+|c_P|^2\n-2\\operatorname{Re}(c_P^2)\\\\\n&amp;=a_P+3b_P^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor example, the disk potential \\(-\\log(1-|w|^2)\\) has normalized complex curvature component \\(-2\\). Taking \\(u_2=iu_1\\) gives real sectional curvature \\(-2\\), fixing the normalization on complex lines.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a positive semidefinite Hermitian form \\(P\\), \\( a_P\\ge b_P^2. \\) For the vertical form \\(V\\), \\( a_V=b_V^2. \\) If \\(P\\) is horizontal and positive semidefinite, \\(m_P\\) is nonnegative, linear and monotone in \\(P\\) …", "statement": "<p>For a positive semidefinite Hermitian form \\(P\\),\n\\[\na_P\\ge b_P^2.\n\\]\nFor the vertical form \\(V\\),\n\\[\na_V=b_V^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(P\\) is horizontal and positive semidefinite, \\(m_P\\) is nonnegative, linear and monotone in \\(P\\), and\n\\[\nm_P\\ge2\\sqrt{a_Pa_V}.\n\\]\nWhen \\(a_Pa_V&gt;0\\), equality holds exactly when the two real Gram matrices of the pair for \\(P\\) and \\(V\\) are proportional.</p>", "proof": "<p>Complex Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\nP(u_1,u_1)P(u_2,u_2)\\ge|P(u_1,u_2)|^2,\n\\]\nproving \\(a_P\\ge b_P^2\\). Equality holds for \\(V\\) because its complex rank is one.</p><p>Let \\(A,B\\) be the two-by-two real Gram matrices with entries\n\\(\\operatorname{Re}P(u_i,u_j)\\) and\n\\(\\operatorname{Re}V(u_i,u_j)\\), respectively. Then\n\\[\na_P=\\det A,\\qquad a_V=\\det B,\\qquad\nm_P=\\operatorname{tr}(\\operatorname{adj}(A)B).\n\\]\nFor positive definite \\(A\\), put \\(C=A^{-1/2}BA^{-1/2}\\). The last expression is\n\\[\n(\\det A)\\operatorname{tr}C,\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\n\\sqrt{a_Pa_V}=(\\det A)\\sqrt{\\det C}.\n\\]\nThe inequality \\(\\operatorname{tr}C\\ge2\\sqrt{\\det C}\\) proves the result, and continuity gives the semidefinite case.</p><p>If both determinants are positive, equality means that \\(C\\) is scalar, exactly the claimed proportionality. The displayed formula for \\(m_P\\) is linear in the entries of \\(P\\). Nonnegativity for positive semidefinite \\(P\\) also proves monotonicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.4 and the block identities immediately preceding it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume \\(T_4,T_3\\) have the component types and bounds in Proposition 7.", "statement": "<p>Assume \\(T_4,T_3\\) have the component types and bounds in Proposition 7. With the notation of Proposition 8,\n\\[\n\\|\\Xi_{HH}\\|^2=2(a_I+b_I^2)\\le4a_I,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\Xi_{H\\bar0}\\|^2=m_I+2b_Ib_V,\\qquad\n\\|\\Xi_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\|^2=2(m_I+2b_Ib_V)\\le4m_I.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{T_4}|\\le Cxa_I,\\qquad\n|\\mathcal H_{T_3}|\\le C\\sqrt q\\,\\sqrt{a_Im_I},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{T_3}|\n\\le C\\sqrt q\\,\\|\\Xi_{HH}\\|\\,\\|\\Xi_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\|.\n\\]\nThese estimates remain valid when a projected real area vanishes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct expansion of \\(\\Xi\\) gives the block identities. For the mixed identity, expand\n\\[\n\\|u_{1,H}\\overline{u_{2,0}}-u_{2,H}\\overline{u_{1,0}}\\|^2.\n\\]\nThe final inequality follows from Lemma 8.2:\n\\[\n|b_Ib_V|\\le\\sqrt{a_Ia_V}\\le m_I/2.\n\\]</p><p>In the contraction formula of Lemma 8.1, \\(T_4\\) pairs two horizontal blocks of \\(\\Xi\\), whereas \\(T_3\\) pairs one horizontal block and one mixed block because it has exactly three horizontal indices. Cauchy–Schwarz and the tensor norm bounds give\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{T_4}|\\le Cx\\|\\Xi_{HH}\\|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{T_3}|\n\\le C\\sqrt q\\,\\|\\Xi_{HH}\\|\\,\\|\\Xi_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\|.\n\\]\nThe block estimates yield the remaining conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "mixed real-plane algebra", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(h\\) be a positive definite horizontal Hermitian form on \\(\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C\\), and let \\(E\\) be a horizontal Hermitian form satisfying \\(E\\ge h\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(h\\) be a positive definite horizontal Hermitian form on \\(\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C\\), and let \\(E\\) be a horizontal Hermitian form satisfying \\(E\\ge h\\). Let \\(u_1,u_2\\) be real-linearly independent, and use the quantities \\(a_P,b_P,m_P,\\Xi\\) of Proposition 8. Define\n\\[\nP_h(E)=a_h+3b_h^2+4a_V+m_E+6b_Eb_V.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(a_ha_V=0\\), then \\(P_h(E)&gt;0\\). If \\(a_ha_V&gt;0\\), put\n\\[\n\\tau=\\frac{|b_h|}{\\sqrt{a_h}}.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(\\beta\\) such that \\(E\\le(2-\\beta)h\\),\n\\[\nP_h(E)\\ge\n\\left(\\sqrt{a_h+3b_h^2}-2\\sqrt{a_V}\\right)^2\n+2\\beta(1+\\tau)\\sqrt{a_ha_V}.\n\\]</p><p>In particular, \\(h\\le E\\le2h\\) implies \\(P_h(E)\\ge0\\). If equality holds, then\n\\[\na_h=a_V&gt;0,\\qquad |b_h|=\\sqrt{a_h},\\qquad b_Eb_V&lt;0,\n\\]\nand both mixed blocks \\(\\Xi^{i\\bar0}\\) and \\(\\Xi^{0\\bar i}\\) vanish.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(a_V=0\\), then \\(b_V=0\\), since the vertical factor has complex dimension one. If \\(a_h=0\\), the horizontal projections are linearly dependent over \\(\\mathbb R\\), since \\(h\\) is positive definite. Their pairing under every Hermitian form is therefore real, so \\(b_E=0\\). Thus \\(a_ha_V=0\\) implies \\(b_Eb_V=0\\), and\n\\[\nP_h(E)\\ge a_h+4a_V+m_h\\ge a_{h+V}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe last inequality uses positive definiteness of \\(h+V\\) and real linear independence.</p><p>Suppose \\(A=a_h&gt;0\\) and \\(U=a_V&gt;0\\). The Hermitian Gram determinant gives \\(0\\le\\tau\\le1\\). Make a real change of basis in the plane so that the horizontal projections \\(u_H,v_H\\) are real-orthonormal for \\(h\\). Both\n\\[\n|b_h|/\\sqrt{a_h},\\qquad |b_E|/\\sqrt{a_h}\n\\]\nare unchanged. The squared \\(h\\)-norms of \\(u_H+iv_H\\) and \\(u_H-iv_H\\) are \\(2(1+\\tau)\\) and \\(2(1-\\tau)\\), in some order. The absolute difference of their squared \\(E\\)-norms is \\(4|b_E|\\) in the new basis.</p><p>Comparing one norm from above and the other from below gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{|b_E|}{\\sqrt A}\n&amp;\\le\\frac{(2-\\beta)(1+\\tau)-(1-\\tau)}2\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{1+3\\tau}2-\\frac{\\beta(1+\\tau)}2\\\\\n&amp;\\le\\sqrt{1+3\\tau^2}-\\frac{\\beta(1+\\tau)}2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor either order of the two norms, the displayed right side is an upper bound, since \\(2-\\beta\\ge1\\). The last inequality follows from\n\\[\n1+3\\tau^2-\\left(\\frac{1+3\\tau}2\\right)^2\n=\\frac34(1-\\tau)^2;\n\\]\nit is strict unless \\(\\tau=1\\). This argument does not require \\(\\beta\\ge0\\).</p><p>Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\nm_E\\ge2\\sqrt{a_EU}\\ge2|b_E|\\sqrt U.\n\\]\nSince \\(U=b_V^2\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_h(E)\n&amp;\\ge A+3b_h^2+4U-4|b_E|\\sqrt U\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\n\\left(\\sqrt{A+3b_h^2}-2\\sqrt U\\right)^2\n+2\\beta(1+\\tau)\\sqrt{AU}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>For the equality case with \\(h\\le E\\le2h\\), the zero-area case shows \\(A,U&gt;0\\). Set\n\\[\ns=\\sqrt{A+3b_h^2},\\qquad B=|b_E|.\n\\]\nThe preceding inequalities with \\(\\beta=0\\) give\n\\[\nP_h(E)\\ge(s-2\\sqrt U)^2+4\\sqrt U(s-B)\\ge0.\n\\]\nEquality forces \\(s=2\\sqrt U\\), \\(B=s\\), and equality in the bounds used above. Strictness in the estimate for \\(B\\) gives \\(\\tau=1\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nU=A,\\qquad B=2\\sqrt A,\\qquad\nb_Eb_V&lt;0,\\qquad m_E=2B\\sqrt U.\n\\]\nSince \\(a_E\\ge B^2\\) and \\(m_E\\ge2\\sqrt{a_EU}\\), these identities force \\(a_E=B^2\\) and equality in the mixed Gram inequality. The horizontal real \\(E\\)-Gram matrix and vertical real Gram matrix are proportional.</p><p>In the real \\(h\\)-orthonormalized basis, \\(\\tau=1\\) says that the horizontal projections have the form\n\\[\nu_H,\\ \\sigma iu_H,\\qquad \\sigma\\in\\{1,-1\\}.\n\\]\nTheir real \\(E\\)-Gram matrix is a positive scalar multiple of the identity. Proportionality implies that the vertical projections have equal positive norms and real inner product zero. The sign \\(b_Eb_V&lt;0\\) selects the opposite complex orientation, so they have the form\n\\[\ny,\\ -\\sigma iy.\n\\]\nDirect substitution gives\n\\[\n\\Xi^{i\\bar0}\n=u_H^i\\overline{-\\sigma iy}-(\\sigma iu_H^i)\\bar y=0.\n\\]\nThe other mixed block vanishes as well. Under a real change of basis in the plane, \\(\\Xi\\) is multiplied by the determinant of the change, so vanishing holds in the original basis.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "uniform negativity for large \\(\\lambda\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<c\\le C<\\infty\\) and \\(0\\le C_e<\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;c\\le C&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(0\\le C_e&lt;\\infty\\). There is a finite\n\\(\\lambda_0=\\lambda_0(c,C,C_e)&gt;0\\) with the following property.</p><p>Let \\(\\lambda\\ge\\lambda_0\\), \\(x\\ge0\\), and let \\(h\\) be a horizontal Hermitian form with\n\\[\ncI\\le h\\le CI.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nq=x(1+x),\\qquad G=\\lambda I+xh,\\qquad\nD=(1+2x)h-qhG^{-1}h.\n\\]\nLet \\(T_4,T_3\\) be tensors with the Kähler symmetries and reality condition, supported respectively on components with exactly four and exactly three horizontal indices, and satisfying\n\\[\n\\|T_4\\|\\le C_ex,\\qquad\n\\|T_3\\|\\le C_e\\sqrt q.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nR=-\\lambda S_I-qS_h-S_V\n-(S_{D+V}-S_D-S_V)+T_4+T_3.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_R&lt;0\n\\]\non every real two-plane, with \\(\\mathcal H_R\\) as in Proposition 8.</p>", "proof": "<p>Increase the prospective threshold so that \\(\\lambda\\ge\\max(1,C)\\). The algebra in Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\n(1+x)h\\le D\\le(1+2x)h.\n\\]</p><p>If the proposition were false, there would be a sequence \\(\\lambda\\to\\infty\\) of permitted data and real two-planes for which \\(F=-\\mathcal H_R\\le0\\). Suppress the sequence index. By Proposition 8,\n\\[\nF=\\lambda(a_I+3b_I^2)+q(a_h+3b_h^2)\n+4a_V+m_D+6b_Db_V\n-\\mathcal H_{T_4}-\\mathcal H_{T_3}.\n\\]\nThe contracted estimates of Proposition 8 apply to the allowed error tensors by their component types and symmetries.</p><p>First suppose a subsequence satisfies \\(q/\\lambda\\to0\\). Monotonicity and linearity of the mixed Gram term give\n\\[\nm_D\\ge c(1+x)m_I,\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\n|b_D|\\le\\sqrt{a_D}\\le C'(1+x)\\sqrt{a_I}.\n\\]\nDiscarding nonnegative terms gives\n\\[\nF\\ge\\lambda a_I+4a_V+c(1+x)m_I\n-C'\\left((1+x)\\sqrt{a_Ia_V}+xa_I+\\sqrt q\\,\\sqrt{a_Im_I}\\right).\n\\]\nAbsorb the first product into \\(2a_V\\), and the last into\n\\(\\frac c2(1+x)m_I\\). Since \\(q/(1+x)=x\\),\n\\[\nF\\ge\n[\\lambda-C''((1+x)^2+x)]a_I\n+2a_V+\\frac c2(1+x)m_I.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n(1+x)^2+x=O(1+q)=o(\\lambda).\n\\]\nEvery coefficient is eventually positive, whereas\n\\[\na_I+a_V+m_I=a_{I+V}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis contradicts \\(F\\le0\\).</p><p>If the preceding regime has no subsequence, pass to one on which \\(q/\\lambda\\) is bounded below by a positive constant. Then \\(\\lambda/q\\) is bounded and \\(x\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>To balance the four-horizontal term \\(qS_h\\) with the vertical term \\(S_V\\), multiply horizontal vector components by \\(q^{1/4}\\). In the resulting plane choose a real-orthonormal pair for \\(\\operatorname{Re}(I+V)\\). The inverse transformation and a real basis change give a basis of the original plane; the latter change multiplies \\(F\\) by a positive factor. Its sign is unchanged. The rescaled pairs belong to a fixed compact Stiefel manifold, with \\(a_{I+V}=1\\).</p><p>In the rescaled variables, the leading part is\n\\[\nL_x=\\frac{\\lambda}{q}(a_I+3b_I^2)+P_h(E),\n\\qquad E=\\frac D{\\sqrt q}.\n\\]\nThe transformed errors satisfy\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde T_4\\|\\le C'\\frac xq=O(x^{-1}),\n\\qquad\n\\|\\widetilde T_3\\|\\le C'q^{-1/4}=O(x^{-1/2}),\n\\]\nbecause each horizontal slot supplies \\(q^{-1/4}\\).</p><p>The lower bound on \\(D\\) gives \\(E\\ge h\\). On an eigendirection of \\(h\\) with eigenvalue \\(d\\in[c,C]\\),\n\\[\n\\frac Eh\n=\\frac{1+2x}{\\sqrt q}\n-\\frac{\\sqrt q\\,d}{\\lambda+xd}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\frac{1+2x}{\\sqrt q}\n=\\sqrt{4+\\frac1q}\\le2+\\frac{C_1}{x^2},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\sqrt q\\,d}{\\lambda+xd}\n\\ge c_1\\frac x{\\lambda+x},\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\nh\\le E\\le\n\\left(2+\\frac{C_1}{x^2}\n-c_1\\frac x{\\lambda+x}\\right)h.\n\\]</p><p>Pass to a subsequence on which \\(h,E,\\lambda/q\\), and the normalized pair converge. At the limit,\n\\[\nh\\le E\\le2h.\n\\]\nThe errors tend to zero. Proposition 9 makes both limiting summands of \\(L_x\\) nonnegative. Since \\(F\\le0\\), both vanish. In particular \\(P_h(E)=0\\) at the limit, so \\(a_h,a_V&gt;0\\), and the mixed blocks of the limiting \\(\\Xi\\) vanish.</p><p>The real Gram-matrix comparisons give\n\\[\nc^2a_I\\le a_h\\le C^2a_I.\n\\]\nHence \\(a_I,a_h,a_V\\) are bounded below by positive constants along this subsequence.</p><p>The three-horizontal error now has an improved evaluated estimate. By the contraction formula in Proposition 8,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H_{\\widetilde T_3}|\n\\le C'q^{-1/4}\\|\\Xi_{HH}\\|\\|\\Xi_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\|\n=o(x^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nThe horizontal block is bounded by compactness of the normalized pairs, and the mixed blocks tend to zero by Proposition 9. The explicit tensor factor \\(q^{-1/4}\\) gives this little-\\(o\\) estimate; no rate of convergence of the planes is assumed.</p><p>Apply Proposition 9 before taking the limit, with\n\\[\n\\beta=c_1\\frac x{\\lambda+x}-\\frac{C_1}{x^2}.\n\\]\nThis is permitted by the bound on \\(E\\), including when \\(\\beta&lt;0\\). Positive lower bounds for \\(a_I,a_h,a_V\\), and boundedness of the normalized pairs, imply\n\\[\nL_x\\ge c_2\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{q}+\\frac x{\\lambda+x}\\right)\n-\\frac{C_2}{x^2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\ge1\\), if \\(\\lambda\\ge x^{3/2}\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda}{q}\\ge\\frac{x^{3/2}}{2x^2}\n=\\frac1{2\\sqrt x}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\lambda\\le x^{3/2}\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac x{\\lambda+x}\\ge\\frac1{\\sqrt x+1}\n\\ge\\frac1{2\\sqrt x}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L_x\\) is bounded below by a positive constant times \\(x^{-1/2}\\) for large \\(x\\). The evaluated \\(\\widetilde T_4\\) contribution is \\(O(x^{-1})=o(x^{-1/2})\\), and the improved estimate gives the same little-\\(o\\) comparison for \\(\\widetilde T_3\\). Hence \\(F&gt;0\\) eventually, a contradiction.</p><p>The two regimes exhaust all failure sequences. This proves the finite threshold uniformly over all permitted matrices, error tensors, and real planes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Hartogs metric transfer", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb B,\\mathbb R)\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb B,\\mathbb R)\\). Put\n\\[\n\\psi=-\\log(1-|z|^2),\\qquad I=\\partial\\bar\\partial\\psi,\n\\qquad h=\\partial\\bar\\partial\\phi.\n\\]\nAssume fixed \\(0&lt;c\\le C&lt;\\infty\\) satisfy, in every centered chart \\(T\\) of Proposition 4,\n\\[\nc\\operatorname{Id}\\le h^T(0)\\le C\\operatorname{Id},\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\partial_kh^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n+|\\partial_{\\bar l}h^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n+|\\partial_k\\partial_{\\bar l}h^T_{i\\bar j}(0)|\n\\le C\n\\qquad(i,j,k,l\\in\\{1,2\\}).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nM_\\phi=\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb B\\times\\mathbb C:\nt=e^{\\phi(z)}|w|^2&lt;1\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_\\lambda=\\lambda\\psi-\\log(1-t),\\qquad\n\\widehat g_\\lambda=\\partial\\bar\\partial\\mathcal K_\\lambda,\n\\qquad g_\\lambda=2\\operatorname{Re}\\widehat g_\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>There is a finite \\(\\lambda_0=\\lambda_0(c,C)&gt;0\\) such that, for every \\(\\lambda\\ge\\lambda_0\\), \\(M_\\phi\\) is contractible and \\(g_\\lambda\\) is a smooth geodesically complete Kähler metric. For this fixed \\(\\lambda\\), there are finite constants\n\\(0&lt;A_\\lambda\\le B_\\lambda\\) such that\n\\[\n-B_\\lambda\\le K_{g_\\lambda}(\\sigma)\\le-A_\\lambda&lt;0\n\\]\nat every point and on every real two-plane.</p>", "proof": "<p>The hypotheses imply \\(h\\ge cI&gt;0\\), so Proposition 6 proves the global geometric assertions for every \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\).</p><p>By Proposition 7, the actual curvature errors have the types and bounds of Proposition 10, with \\(C_e\\) depending only on the chart bounds. Choose a finite \\(\\lambda\\ge\\lambda_0\\) from Proposition 10. Then\n\\[\n-\\mathcal H_R&gt;0\n\\]\non every real two-plane. It remains to bound the quotient of Proposition 8 uniformly away from zero and negative infinity.</p><p>First restrict \\(x=t/(1-t)\\) to a bounded interval \\([0,X]\\), and normalize pairs for \\(\\operatorname{Re}(I+V)\\). The data\n\\[\nx,\\ h,\\ T_4,\\ T_3,\\ \\text{normalized pair}\n\\]\nrange over a closed bounded subset of a finite-dimensional space: impose\n\\[\ncI\\le h\\le CI,\n\\]\nthe component types and symmetries, and the closed bounds\n\\[\n\\|T_4\\|\\le C_ex,\\qquad \\|T_3\\|\\le C_e\\sqrt q.\n\\]\nThis set is compact. The formulas for \\(G,D,R\\) are continuous. Proposition 10 applies to the whole set, so \\(-\\mathcal H_R\\) has a strictly positive minimum and a finite maximum. The metric \\(G+V\\) is uniformly positive definite and bounded there, so \\(a_{G+V}\\) is bounded above and away from zero. Proposition 8 gives both curvature bounds for \\(0\\le x\\le X\\).</p><p>For \\(x\\to\\infty\\), use the horizontal rescaling of Proposition 10, now with \\(\\lambda\\) fixed. The rescaled Hermitian metric is\n\\[\n\\widetilde g_x=G/\\sqrt q+V.\n\\]\nUniformly in the permitted data,\n\\[\n\\frac G{\\sqrt q}-h\n=\\frac{\\lambda}{\\sqrt q}I\n+\\left(\\frac x{\\sqrt q}-1\\right)h\n=O_\\lambda(x^{-1}).\n\\]\nSince \\(G\\) commutes with \\(h\\), the formula for \\(D\\) also gives\n\\[\nD=xh+\\lambda(1+x)hG^{-1}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\frac D{\\sqrt q}-h\n=\\left(\\frac x{\\sqrt q}-1\\right)h\n+\\frac{\\lambda(1+x)}{\\sqrt q}hG^{-1}\n=O_\\lambda(x^{-1}).\n\\]</p><p>The rescaled tensor identity therefore implies\n\\[\n\\widetilde R_x=-S_{h+V}+O_\\lambda(x^{-1/2})\n\\]\nin component norm, uniformly over all allowed data. Indeed, \\(\\lambda S_I\\) is divided by \\(q\\), \\(qS_h\\) becomes exactly \\(S_h\\), the mixed term converges by the estimate for \\(D/\\sqrt q\\), and the transformed errors obey the bounds in Proposition 10.</p><p>For the limiting metric \\(h+V\\) and tensor \\(-S_{h+V}\\), Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\nK=-\\frac12\\left(1+3\\frac{b_{h+V}^2}{a_{h+V}}\\right)\n\\in[-2,-1/2].\n\\]\nFor an \\((I+V)\\)-real-orthonormal pair,\n\\[\na_{h+V}\\ge\\min(c,1)^2.\n\\]\nThus the limiting area denominators are uniformly bounded away from zero, and the forms \\(h+V\\) range over a compact bounded family. The metric and tensor estimates imply uniform convergence of the sectional-curvature quotients over all real planes.</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(X_\\lambda&lt;\\infty\\), this gives, for example,\n\\[\n-3\\le K_g\\le-1/4\\qquad(x\\ge X_\\lambda).\n\\]\nCombine this with the compact-range bounds at \\(X=X_\\lambda\\). The zero-section tensor was computed in Proposition 7 in a smooth normalized fiber frame; equivalently the bounds extend there by smoothness. This proves both uniform finite negative bounds.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 5, "cite"], [1, 11, "cite"], [1, 13, "cite"], [1, 14, "cite"], [1, 16, "cite"], [1, 18, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [5, 12, "cite"], [5, 13, "cite"], [5, 14, "cite"], [5, 16, "cite"], [5, 17, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 5, "cite"], [9, 5, "cite"], [10, 5, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [11, 17, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 21, "cite"], [14, 28, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 14, "cite"], [18, 14, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [20, 0, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 28, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 25, "cite"], [21, 27, "cite"], [21, 28, "cite"], [22, 26, "cite"], [22, 27, "cite"], [22, 28, "cite"], [23, 22, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [24, 22, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 22, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 0, "cite"]]});
